---
Part V: Tangling
Chapter 1: The compile layer
Package: packages/loom-lang/
Language: TypeScript
---

# The compile layer

Loom compiles a `.loom` file from its own syntax into code. The **Loom syntax** — the headings, anchors, and sinks — says how the file composes, and Loom serves it on the source itself. The **product** is the author's code, composed from the sections those anchors name. `LoomCompiler` is where the two meet: it runs the chain — `parse → product` — and projects the result for whoever asks.

## The chain and its verbs

`LoomCompiler` is the higher-order service that runs the chain. It composes the passes rather than re-implementing them, and hands each consumer a finished projection through a verb.

Its spine is one step. `buildCorpus` runs `parse |> product`: it loads each module through `LoomCorpusAstBuilder` — which parses the file and composes its product in the same pass — keeps it in `LoomMemo`, and discovers the rest of the corpus beside it. The corpus is the through-line every projection reads.

A module this process has never built may still be waiting on disk. `loadOne` hands each build to `LoomDiskCache`, which returns the module an earlier process kept for the same loom and settings, and builds and keeps it otherwise. So a fresh language server or tangle reads most of the corpus back rather than parsing it again.

The product is built once, with the parse. `LoomCorpusAstBuilder` composes each module's product as it loads, so `LoomMemo` caches the whole module — parse and product together — and a second projection of an unedited file reuses it. An edit evicts the edited file alone, so the next projection rebuilds only what changed. A file that draws in the edited one keeps its build, because a cross-file anchor is inlined when a file is projected, not when it is built.

The verbs project the corpus for the consumers. `compile` gives the editor its Volar tree, the source as the `loom` root and the product sections beneath it. `tangle` resolves a file's `{path}` sinks to the content and target the CLI writes. `diagnose` gives a file's Loom health, the diagnostics the editor shows. `definition` follows an anchor to the section it names, and `references` finds every anchor that names the section under the cursor. `rename` gathers the title and anchors a rename rewrites together, and `renameRange` gives the span the rename box covers, while `navigationRange` gives the whole span a definition link underlines over any navigable token. `semanticTokens` gives each of a file's tokens its colour, so the editor highlights the source. `hints` gives the title beside each part or chapter an anchor names by number, so the editor shows where a numbered reference points. `boxes` gives each box of a file with its caption and the lines it spans, so the editor folds the box under its name. `summaryEdits` keeps a box and the loom it points to in step as the author types. Given a loom's text before an edit and after it, it parses both, asks the corpus for the edits that carry the change across, and gives each as a `SummaryEdit`: the loom, the range in the editor's coordinates, and the text. `completions` gives the labels an anchor takes while the cursor stands between its braces, each with the span it replaces, so the editor completes a label as the author types it. `contracts` gives what each anchor says its section provides: where the section's code stands, and for each name, the `MissingSymbol` diagnostic the editor raises when the section's language service reports no such name there. `promised` gives the names a root is promised before anyone writes the section that provides them, so a language service can lower its error for such a name to a warning. `reach` gives the files an entry pulls in, so the editor associates their scripts. `weave` projects the whole corpus into a `WovenCorpus`, the pages and navigation the website renders. `contents` reads each chapter's frontmatter and rewrites the book's own table of contents from it, returning the rewritten source. `invalidate` evicts a file and names what an edit to it must reproject: the file and every file that draws it in. A file the cache does not hold has no build to evict, and no projection holds its old text, so `invalidate` names nothing for it. `invalidateWithin` evicts every file the cache holds under a folder and names them, for a change that reaches every loom there at once, such as a change to the folder's configuration. `within` decides which files lie under a folder. It joins the folder with the platform's separator, so a trailing slash makes no difference, and a sibling folder whose name merely starts the same, `inside2` beside `inside`, is not under it. `rebuild` builds a file again from the text a source gives, and says whether what other files see of it changed.

Every verb builds the corpus the same way and differs only in what it reads. `compile` and `tangle` read the product — the composed sections and the sink files. `diagnose`, `definition`, `references`, `rename`, `renameRange`, `navigationRange`, `semanticTokens`, `hints`, `completions`, `reach`, `weave`, `contents`, and `summaries` read the parse and the corpus graph, since Loom health, navigation, colour, completion, placement, the woven page, the table of contents, and the summaries that boxes copy need no product. `contracts` reads the product too, since a contract binds the anchor's edge to the section it reaches, and so does `promised`, since a promise rides on the edges a root's composition follows. `compile` takes its `Source` from the caller, so the editor reads the open buffer.

The build cache keys a module by its path alone, so it can hold a build of the entry from text the editor no longer shows. The corpus may have been built from disk before the editor projected a chapter it has open: by the language server warming the corpus at start, or by a composition the TypeScript service asked for. That build holds the saved text, while the editor's buffer may hold edits that were never saved. So `compile` first runs `matchEntry`, which compares the kept build of the entry with the text its source gives and evicts the build when the two differ. A source that cannot read the entry leaves the build alone, and the build reports the error as it always did. The entry is then built from the buffer, and the editor never answers from text it is not showing.

`compile` also guards Volar's synchronous hook: a defect degrades to a bare `loom` document carrying the file's text, and the editor keeps working rather than crashing. The other verbs read through `DocumentSource`.

An anchor of the `Reference` kind refers to a module rather than composing a section, as the chapter "Referring to modules" describes, and only the module's language package can write the path to it. The compiler reaches the packages through the `LanguagePackages` service when the environment provides one. The CLI's environment loads a workspace's packages the first time it asks, and the language server holds the ones it loaded for its plugins. `specifierOf` asks a package to write the path a reference names, and `writtenFor` asks it for every reference a root holds, as the section "Writing a reference's path" describes. With no `LanguagePackages`, as in a test or over remote storage, a reference's path stays unwritten.

A workspace may install tropes from its store, as the chapter "Tropes a workspace installs" describes, and their declared kinds join the registry every judgement reads. The compiler reaches them through the `TropePackages` service when the environment provides one, the same way it reaches the language packages. `installedKinds` asks it for the kinds the workspace of a loom installs. With no `TropePackages`, as in a test or over remote storage, a workspace installs no trope.

Every build passes through `LoomDiskCache`, which may keep the module on disk for the next process. The compiler's layer provides its passes and the cache in memory, but not this one. The environment provides it, as the chapter "Where a workspace is kept" describes, so a workspace on a disk keeps its modules there, and a workspace in remote storage keeps none. The cache must never keep a module built from text that was not saved, so `loadOne` hands it `DocumentSource` beside the source the build reads. The cache checks the module against the saved text that `DocumentSource` gives.

=>

export class LoomCompiler extends Context.Service<LoomCompiler>()('LoomCompiler', {
  make: Effect.gen(function* () {
    const documents = yield* DocumentSource
    const workspaces = yield* WorkspaceSource
    const builder = yield* LoomCorpusAstBuilder
    const memo = yield* LoomMemo
    const disk = yield* LoomDiskCache
    const config = yield* WorkspaceSettings
    const weaver = yield* WeaveBuilder
    const tocBuilder = yield* TableOfContentsBuilder
    const composed = yield* Ref.make<Composition>(noComposition)
    const packages = yield* Effect.serviceOption(LanguagePackages)
    const tropes = yield* Effect.serviceOption(TropePackages)

    const installedKinds = (loom: Location): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<DeclaredKind>> =>
      Option.match(tropes, {
        onNone: () => Effect.succeed<ReadonlyArray<DeclaredKind>>([]),
        onSome: (installed) => installed.kindsFor(loom),
      })

    const specifierOf = (request: SpecifierRequest): Effect.Effect<Option.Option<string>, ServiceError> =>
      Option.match(packages, {
        onNone: () => Effect.succeed(Option.none<string>()),
        onSome: (held) => held.moduleSpecifier(request),
      })

    const writtenFor: WrittenFor = (modules, book, landing, root, from) =>
      Effect.forEach(referencesIn(modules, root, book), (reference) =>
        pipe(
          Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(reference.owner)),
          Option.map((module) =>
            referredFileOf(book, modules, landing, module, reference.host, reference.ref),
          ),
          Option.filter(Referred.$is('Named')),
          Option.match({
            onNone: () => Effect.succeed(nothingWritten),
            onSome: ({ landed }) =>
              workspaces.placeOf(landed.loom, landed.file.path).pipe(
                Effect.flatMap((to) =>
                  specifierOf({
                    language: reference.host.languageId,
                    loom: reference.owner,
                    from,
                    to,
                  }),
                ),
                Effect.map(
                  Option.match({
                    onNone: () => unwrittenOf(reference, 'No package writes its paths.'),
                    onSome: (text) => writtenOf(reference, text),
                  }),
                ),
                Effect.catchTag('ServiceError', (error) =>
                  Effect.succeed(unwrittenOf(reference, error.reason)),
                ),
              ),
          }),
        ),
      ).pipe(Effect.map(Array.reduce(nothingWritten, mergeWritten)))

    const fileOfRoot = (entry: LoomModule, root: Code): Effect.Effect<Location> =>
      Option.match(
        Array.findFirst(entry.product.files, (file) => file.code.origin.name === root.origin.name),
        {
          onNone: () => Effect.succeed(`${entry.path}.${root.origin.name.toLowerCase()}`),
          onSome: (file) => workspaces.placeOf(entry.path, file.path),
        },
      )

    const rootSpecifiers = (
      modules: Modules,
      entry: LoomModule,
    ): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyMap<string, Specifiers>> => {
      if (!holdsReferences(modules)) return Effect.succeed(new Map())
      const book = bookOf(modules)
      const landing = landingOf(book, modules)
      return Effect.forEach(entry.product.code, (root) =>
        fileOfRoot(entry, root).pipe(
          Effect.flatMap((from) => writtenFor(modules, book, landing, root, from)),
          Effect.map((written) => [root.origin.name, specifiersOf(written)] as const),
        ),
      ).pipe(Effect.map((pairs) => new Map(pairs)))
    }

    const loadOne = (source: Source, path: Location): Effect.Effect<LoomModule> =>
      config
        .resolve(path)
        .pipe(
          Effect.flatMap((settings) =>
            memo.get(
              path,
              disk.through(
                source,
                documents,
                path,
                settings,
                builder.build(
                  source,
                  path,
                  settings.delims,
                  settings.primaryLanguage,
                  settings.variables,
                ),
              ),
            ),
          ),
        )

    const corpusPaths = (source: Source, entry: Location): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<Location>> =>
      Option.match(source.list, {
        onNone: () => Effect.succeed<ReadonlyArray<Location>>([entry]),
        onSome: (list) => list(entry).pipe(Effect.map((found) => Array.dedupe([entry, ...found]))),
      })

    const matchEntry = (source: Source, path: Location): Effect.Effect<void> =>
      Effect.all([Effect.option(source.read(path)), memo.entries]).pipe(
        Effect.flatMap(([text, modules]) =>
          pipe(
            Option.all([text, Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path))]),
            Option.filter(([shown, kept]) => kept.text !== shown),
            Option.match({ onNone: () => Effect.void, onSome: () => memo.evict([path]) }),
          ),
        ),
      )

    const buildCorpus = (source: Source, entry: Location): Effect.Effect<Modules> =>
      corpusPaths(source, entry).pipe(
        Effect.flatMap((paths) =>
          Effect.forEach(paths, (path) =>
            loadOne(source, path).pipe(Effect.map((m) => [path, m] as const)),
          ),
        ),
        Effect.map((pairs) => new Map(pairs)),
      )

    return {
      compile: (source: Source, path: Location): Effect.Effect<VirtualCode> =>
        matchEntry(source, path).pipe(
          Effect.andThen(buildCorpus(source, path)),
          Effect.flatMap((modules) => entryOf(modules, path)),
          Effect.flatMap(({ modules, entry }) =>
            rootSpecifiers(modules, entry).pipe(
              Effect.map((specifiers) => toVolar(projectTree(modules, entry, specifiers))),
            ),
          ),
          Effect.catchCause((cause) =>
            Effect.logError('loom: projection failed; serving bare document', cause).pipe(
              Effect.andThen(
                source.read(path).pipe(
                  Effect.orElseSucceed(() => ''),
                  Effect.map((text) => toVolar(rootVirtualCode(text, []))),
                ),
              ),
            ),
          ),
        ),

      tangle: (path: Location): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<TangledFile>, TangleError> =>
        Effect.all([buildCorpus(documents, path), installedKinds(path)]).pipe(
          Effect.flatMap(([modules, installed]) => {
            const scoped = scopedTo(modules, reachable(modules, path))
            const failures = [
              ...corpusErrors({ modules: scoped }, corpusDiagnostics(modules, installed)),
              ...unsettledFailures(modules, path),
            ]
            return failures.length > 0
              ? Effect.fail(new TangleError({ entry: path, failures }))
              : resolveSinks(modules, path, workspaces.placeOf, writtenFor).pipe(
                  Effect.flatMap(({ files, unwritten }) =>
                    Array.isReadonlyArrayNonEmpty(unwritten)
                      ? Effect.fail(new TangleError({ entry: path, failures: byLoom(unwritten) }))
                      : Effect.succeed(files),
                  ),
                )
          }),
        ),

      composition: (path: Location): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<ComposedFile>> =>
        buildCorpus(documents, path).pipe(
          Effect.flatMap((modules) =>
            Effect.forEach(Array.fromIterable(modules.values()), (module) =>
              Effect.forEach(module.product.files, (file) =>
                workspaces.placeOf(module.path, file.path),
              ).pipe(Effect.map((paths) => [module.path, paths] as const)),
            ).pipe(
              Effect.map((pairs) => new Map(pairs)),
              Effect.flatMap((placed) =>
                Ref.get(composed).pipe(
                  Effect.flatMap((previous) => recompose(previous, modules, placed, writtenFor)),
                  Effect.tap((next) => Ref.set(composed, next)),
                  Effect.map(composedFiles),
                ),
              ),
            ),
          ),
        ),

      weave: (path: Location): Effect.Effect<WovenCorpus> =>
        buildCorpus(documents, path).pipe(Effect.flatMap((modules) => weaver.build({ modules }))),

      contents: (path: Location): Effect.Effect<string> =>
        buildCorpus(documents, path).pipe(
          Effect.flatMap((modules) =>
            tocBuilder
              .build({ modules })
              .pipe(Effect.map((toc) => rewriteBook({ modules }, path, toc))),
          ),
        ),

      summaries: (
        path: Location,
      ): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<{ readonly path: Location; readonly text: string }>> =>
        buildCorpus(documents, path).pipe(Effect.map((modules) => refreshedSummaries({ modules }))),

      summaryEdits: (
        path: Location,
        before: string,
        after: string,
      ): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<SummaryEdit>> =>
        Effect.all([config.resolve(path), buildCorpus(documents, path)]).pipe(
          Effect.flatMap(([settings, modules]) => {
            const parsedAs = (text: string): Effect.Effect<LoomModule> =>
              builder.build(
                { read: () => Effect.succeed(text), list: Option.none() },
                path,
                settings.delims,
                settings.primaryLanguage,
                settings.variables,
              )
            return Effect.all([parsedAs(before), parsedAs(after)]).pipe(
              Effect.map(([was, now]) =>
                Array.getSomes(
                  Array.map(syncedSummaries({ modules }, was, now), (edit) =>
                    Option.map(
                      Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(edit.path)),
                      (target): SummaryEdit => ({
                        path: edit.path,
                        range: {
                          start: lineCharAt(target.text, edit.at.start.offset),
                          end: lineCharAt(target.text, edit.at.end.offset),
                        },
                        text: edit.text,
                      }),
                    ),
                  ),
                ),
              ),
            )
          }),
        ),

      diagnose: (path: Location): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic>> =>
        Effect.all([buildCorpus(documents, path), installedKinds(path)]).pipe(
          Effect.map(([modules, installed]) =>
            pipe(
              Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)),
              Option.match({
                onNone: () => [],
                onSome: (module) => [
                  ...corpusDiagnostics(modules, installed)(module),
                  ...Array.flatMap(
                    unresolvedTocEntriesIn({ modules }, module),
                    (entry) =>
                      faulty(
                        UnresolvedTocEntry({
                          title: entry.title.value,
                          discussion: entry.discussion,
                        }),
                        entry.title.position,
                      ).diagnostics,
                  ),
                ],
              }),
            ),
          ),
        ),

      definition: (path: Location, offset: number): Effect.Effect<FrameLocation | undefined> =>
        buildCorpus(documents, path).pipe(
          Effect.map((modules) =>
            Option.getOrUndefined(
              Option.flatMap(definitionAt({ modules }, path, offset), (loc) =>
                frameLocationOf(modules, loc),
              ),
            ),
          ),
        ),

      references: (path: Location, offset: number): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<FrameLocation>> =>
        buildCorpus(documents, path).pipe(
          Effect.map((modules) =>
            Array.getSomes(
              Array.map(referencesAt({ modules }, path, offset), (loc) =>
                frameLocationOf(modules, loc),
              ),
            ),
          ),
        ),

      rename: (path: Location, offset: number): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<FrameLocation>> =>
        buildCorpus(documents, path).pipe(
          Effect.map((modules) =>
            Array.getSomes(
              Array.map(renameAt({ modules }, path, offset), (loc) =>
                frameLocationOf(modules, loc),
              ),
            ),
          ),
        ),

      renameRange: (path: Location, offset: number): Effect.Effect<FrameLocation | undefined> =>
        buildCorpus(documents, path).pipe(
          Effect.map((modules) =>
            Option.getOrUndefined(
              Option.flatMap(renameRangeAt({ modules }, path, offset), (loc) =>
                frameLocationOf(modules, loc),
              ),
            ),
          ),
        ),

      navigationRange: (path: Location, offset: number): Effect.Effect<FrameLocation | undefined> =>
        buildCorpus(documents, path).pipe(
          Effect.map((modules) =>
            Option.getOrUndefined(
              Option.flatMap(navigationRangeAt({ modules }, path, offset), (loc) =>
                frameLocationOf(modules, loc),
              ),
            ),
          ),
        ),

      semanticTokens: (path: Location): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<FrameToken>> =>
        buildCorpus(documents, path).pipe(
          Effect.map((modules) =>
            pipe(
              Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)),
              Option.match({
                onNone: () => [],
                onSome: (module) =>
                  Array.getSomes(
                    Array.map(symbolsOf(module.doc), (symbol) =>
                      Option.flatMap(profileOf(symbol.kind).semantic, (token) =>
                        Option.map(
                          frameLocationOf(modules, {
                            path,
                            position: symbol.position,
                          }),
                          (location): FrameToken => ({
                            range: location.range,
                            type: token,
                          }),
                        ),
                      ),
                    ),
                  ),
              }),
            ),
          ),
        ),

      boxes: (path: Location): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<FrameBox>> =>
        buildCorpus(documents, path).pipe(
          Effect.map((modules) =>
            Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)), {
              onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<FrameBox> => [],
              onSome: (module) =>
                Array.map(openedBoxesOf(module.doc), (opened): FrameBox => ({
                  startLine: lineCharAt(module.text, opened.opener.position.start.offset).line,
                  endLine: lineCharAt(module.text, lastLineOf(opened).position.start.offset).line,
                  caption: captionOf(opened),
                })),
            }),
          ),
        ),

      hints: (path: Location): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<FrameHint>> =>
        buildCorpus(documents, path).pipe(
          Effect.map((modules) =>
            Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)), {
              onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<FrameHint> => [],
              onSome: (module) =>
                Array.map(hintsAt({ modules }, path), (hint): FrameHint => ({
                  position: lineCharAt(module.text, hint.offset),
                  label: hint.label,
                })),
            }),
          ),
        ),

      completions: (
        path: Location,
        offset: number,
      ): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<FrameCompletion>> =>
        buildCorpus(documents, path).pipe(
          Effect.map((modules) =>
            Option.match(
              Option.all([
                Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)),
                labelSlotAt({ modules }, path, offset),
              ]),
              {
                onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<FrameCompletion> => [],
                onSome: ([module, slot]) =>
                  Array.map(anchorKinds, (label): FrameCompletion => ({
                    label,
                    range: {
                      start: lineCharAt(module.text, slot.start),
                      end: lineCharAt(module.text, slot.end),
                    },
                    text: slot.closed ? label : `${label}}`,
                  })),
              },
            ),
          ),
        ),

      contracts: (path: Location): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<FrameContract>> =>
        buildCorpus(documents, path).pipe(
          Effect.map((modules) =>
            Array.getSomes(
              Array.map(contractsIn(modules, path), (contract) =>
                Option.map(
                  Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(contract.target)),
                  (target): FrameContract => ({
                    target: {
                      path: contract.target,
                      range: {
                        start: lineCharAt(target.text, contract.span.start.offset),
                        end: lineCharAt(target.text, contract.span.end.offset),
                      },
                    },
                    language: contract.language,
                    expected: Array.map(contract.provides, (provided) => ({
                      name: provided.name,
                      diagnostic: faultAt(
                        MissingSymbol({
                          name: provided.name,
                          section: contract.section,
                          ...Option.match(contract.trope, {
                            onNone: () => ({}),
                            onSome: (trope) => ({ trope }),
                          }),
                        }),
                        provided.at,
                      ),
                    })),
                  }),
                ),
              ),
            ),
          ),
        ),

      promised: (path: Location, rootId: string): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<PromisedName>> =>
        buildCorpus(documents, path).pipe(
          Effect.map((modules) => promisedNamesIn(modules, path, rootId)),
        ),

      reach: (path: Location): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<Location>> =>
        buildCorpus(documents, path).pipe(
          Effect.map((modules) => Array.filter(reachable(modules, path), (p) => p !== path)),
        ),

      invalidate: (path: Location): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<Location>> =>
        memo.entries.pipe(
          Effect.flatMap((modules) =>
            modules.has(path)
              ? memo
                  .evict([path])
                  .pipe(Effect.as([path, ...transitiveDependents(modules, path)]))
              : Effect.succeed<ReadonlyArray<Location>>([]),
          ),
        ),

      invalidateWithin: (dir: Location): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<Location>> =>
        memo.entries.pipe(
          Effect.map((modules) =>
            Array.filter(Array.fromIterable(modules.keys()), (path) => within(dir, path)),
          ),
          Effect.tap((paths) => memo.evict(paths)),
        ),

      rebuild: (source: Source, path: Location): Effect.Effect<boolean> =>
        memo.entries.pipe(
          Effect.map((modules) => Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)), faceOf)),
          Effect.flatMap((before) =>
            matchEntry(source, path).pipe(
              Effect.andThen(loadOne(source, path)),
              Effect.map((module) => !Option.contains(before, faceOf(module))),
            ),
          ),
        ),
    }
  }),
}) {
  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(this, this.make).pipe(
    Layer.provide(
      Layer.mergeAll(
        LoomCorpusAstBuilder.layer,
        LoomMemo.layer,
        WeaveBuilder.layer,
        TableOfContentsBuilder.layer,
      ),
    ),
  )
}

## The discovered corpus

A file's corpus is the looms around it that belong to its project, discovered from disk rather than named in imports. `buildCorpus` lists the file's corpus directory through the source and builds each loom the listing returns. So a book reaches the chapters it places and an anchor reaches the section it names, with nothing imported by hand. A source that lists nothing — a single document held in memory — yields a corpus of one.

Which looms share a file's corpus depends on where the looms are kept, so the compiler leaves the answer to the source. `corpusPaths` asks the source's `list` for the looms around the entry, and puts the entry first whether or not the listing names it. `DocumentSource` asks the `WorkspaceSource` service's `corpusOf` method. On the file system, that method applies the one rule every walk over a workspace follows, the rule the chapter on the looms in a project sets out, as the chapter "Where a workspace is kept" describes. So the corpus the editor composes is the corpus `tangle` writes and `orphans` counts.

The whole corpus is what navigation resolves against, so a name reaches a section wherever in the corpus it lives. The tangle and the diagnose narrow it: each scopes to the entry and the sections it composes across files, through `reachable`, so tangling one file never reaches a sibling it should not, and a broken sibling never fails a neighbour's build.

A `Modules` map keys each module by its path. It is the value `buildCorpus` returns and every projection reads. `scopedTo` keeps the modules a set of paths names, the way the tangle narrows the whole corpus to one entry's reach.

=>

type Modules = ReadonlyMap<Location, LoomModule>

const scopedTo = (modules: Modules, paths: ReadonlyArray<Location>): Modules => {
  const keep = new Set(paths)
  return new Map(Array.filter(Array.fromIterable(modules), ([p]) => keep.has(p)))
}

## Resolving the entry

Two verbs are about one file within its corpus, so they pick that module out once the corpus is produced. `entryOf` looks the entry up by path and dies if it is absent — a projection of a file the corpus never loaded is a bug, not a recoverable state. It returns the entry beside the corpus it came from, so a caller reads both from one result.

=>

const entryOf = (
  modules: Modules,
  path: Location,
): Effect.Effect<{ readonly modules: Modules; readonly entry: LoomModule }> =>
  Effect.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)).pipe(
    Effect.orDie,
    Effect.map((entry) => ({ modules, entry })),
  )

## Judging a module against its corpus

A module's diagnostics come from two places. Its own two tiers, the parse and the product, travel with the module in the memo, and `moduleDiagnostics` reads them. The rest are judged against the corpus, because the module alone cannot see them. An anchor that reaches beyond its loom goes to the product pass's `crossFileFaults`, which tells whether the section it names exists. Every composing anchor goes to `compositionCycles` as well, which faults an anchor whose section composes, however far down, the section the anchor stands in. Every section goes to `unusedSections`, which warns of code that no file includes, against the corpus's used sections, which `usedSections` gathers once. A chapter's title goes to `namesakeFaults`, which raises the `DuplicateName` fault when another chapter of its part carries the same title, and names those chapters by their looms through `loomName`.

`corpusDiagnostics` reads both for one module, against the corpus the module came from. It builds the corpus's `Book` once, so the judgement of every module resolves names through the same index, and the corpus's `Landing` once, which tells the file each section lands in, for a reference that names a section with no sink. The tangle gates on it and `diagnose` reports it, so the build and the editor agree on what is broken. A box that points to a loom needs the corpus too, since that loom lives in another file. `boxNoticeHealth` turns each of its notices into a diagnostic at the box's name: a box whose loom nobody has written carries the `UnwrittenLoom` note, a trope box that points nowhere yet carries the `UnsettledTrope` note, and a box whose copy of the summary is out of step carries the `StaleSummary` warning. Neither is an error, so neither stops a tangle.

A box's kind needs the corpus as well, since a trope anywhere in the book may declare it. A trope the workspace installs may declare it too, and the `installed` kinds the compiler hands in carry those. `corpusDiagnostics` gathers the registry of declared kinds once, through `declaredKindsOf`, and `kindFaults` judges each module against it: a box whose kind nobody gives carries the `UnknownKind` fault, and a trope Loom refuses carries the `TropeRefused` fault, as Part XIII, Tropes, describes. Both are errors, so either stops a tangle, as an unknown kind did when the parse alone judged it.

A trope's boundary needs the corpus too, since the chapters that reach a trope may lie anywhere in the book. `corpusDiagnostics` derives every trope's boundary once, through `boundariesOf`, from the tropes the registry gathered, and `boundaryFaults` judges each module against them. A reach across a boundary to a chapter a trope keeps private is an error, and stops a tangle, as do faulty entries and tropes rooted in each other. A trope with no root, and a chapter a trope reaches without the chapter naming it, are notes.

=>

const loomName = (loom: Location): string => loom.slice(loom.lastIndexOf('/') + 1)

const namesakeFaults = (book: Book, module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> =>
  Option.match(namesakesOf(book, module), {
    onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => [],
    onSome: ({ title, looms }) =>
      faulty(
        DuplicateName({
          name: title.source,
          sibling: 'chapter',
          where: Array.join(Array.map(looms, loomName), ', '),
        }),
        title.position,
      ).diagnostics,
  })

const boxNoticeHealth = (notice: BoxNotice): Health =>
  BoxNotice.$match(notice, {
    Unwritten: ({ name, kind, opening }) =>
      faulty(UnwrittenLoom({ kind, name: name.value, opening }), name.position),
    Unsettled: ({ name, target }) =>
      faulty(
        UnsettledTrope({
          name: name.value,
          ...Option.match(target, { onNone: () => ({}), onSome: (named) => ({ target: named }) }),
        }),
        name.position,
      ),
    Stale: ({ name, of, title, summary, body }) =>
      faulty(StaleSummary({ of, name: title, summary, at: body }), name.position),
  })

const corpusDiagnostics = (modules: Modules, installed: ReadonlyArray<DeclaredKind>) => {
  const book = bookOf(modules)
  const landing = landingOf(book, modules)
  const kinds = declaredKindsOf(modules, book, installed)
  const boundaries = boundariesOf(modules, book, kinds.tropes)
  const used = usedSections(book, modules)
  return (module: LoomModule): ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> => [
    ...moduleDiagnostics(module),
    ...crossFileFaults(book, modules, module, landing),
    ...compositionCycles(book, modules, module),
    ...unusedSections(used, module),
    ...namesakeFaults(book, module),
    ...kindFaults(kinds, module),
    ...boundaryFaults(boundaries, book, modules, module),
    ...Array.flatMap(
      boxNoticesIn({ modules }, module),
      (notice) => boxNoticeHealth(notice).diagnostics,
    ),
  ]
}

## Tangling a file

`tangle` is the product's file projection, the sibling of `compile`. It produces the corpus, refuses a broken build, and resolves the entry's `{Tangle}` sections to the content and target a writer emits. It scopes through `reachable` — the entry and the sections it composes — yet emits only the entry's own files, so a loom it merely transcludes never re-emits, and every file is written once.

`composition` is that same resolution turned to the editor: every root in the corpus, the code it composes and the path it composes to, ungated and unscoped. It is the live product as a filesystem — what a product language service builds its analyzer's program over, so a cross-file import resolves against a section's live composition rather than its output on disk.

`resolveSinks` gathers the files this tangle emits: the entry module's own tangle sinks — one per `[dir, file]` section — and every sink a book in the corpus routes into place. For each it resolves where the file lands, writes the paths the file's references name from there through `writtenFor`, and flattens the composed code across the corpus with `fromCode`, over one `Book` built for the whole entry. It starts from the sink's own `Code` rather than finding the section again by its name, so a namesake can never stand in for the sink. A module's file emits only when the entry produced it or a book routes it, so importing a module to transclude its code never re-emits that module's own files. Where a file lands depends on where the looms are kept, so `resolveSinks` asks the `WorkspaceSource` service's `placeOf` method for each sink. On the file system a sink lands under the package root, the folder above the corpus folder, so a sink names its file relative to that root rather than climbing out of the corpus. The formatter reads the same rule, to name the file a root tangles to. `fileRoot` chooses that root for each sink: the chapter prefix a book places the sink under, resolved against the workspace, or the package root the configuration gives when no chapter places it. A file the workspace assigns to no package falls back to the module's own directory. `resolveSinks` returns a `TangledFile` per sink: the section that produced it, where it lands, and its content. `LoomTangler` performs the writes; `tangle` itself touches no disk.

A tangle must never emit an anchor it could not resolve, nor build on a file it could not read. So before it resolves a single sink, `tangle` runs `corpusErrors` over the modules the entry reaches, and fails with a `TangleError` when any of them carries an error. It reads each module through `corpusDiagnostics` over the whole corpus, not only the reached modules, so a name resolves through the same book in the build as in the editor. A malformed parse stops the build. So does an anchor whose name no section of the book carries, a name two places share, and an anchor whose path names a loom that lacks the section. None of them leaves a file with a silently dropped reference. Neither does a reference whose path its package could not write, which fails the tangle after the files are composed. The failure is loud, and names every file and line.

A tangle also refuses a file whose composition reaches a section to be settled, a section that holds no code yet or one an anchor labels `{To be settled}` before anyone declares it. The editor treats such a section as a note, since nothing is wrong while the author writes, but a file that composed one would ship without the code its anchor names. `unsettledFailures` walks the composition of each file the entry tangles through `unsettledIn`, and turns each unsettled reference into the `UnsettledSection` note, grouped by the loom that holds it. A chapter named whole whose opening section holds no code is refused the same way, and so is a reference to a module nobody has written yet, with the `UnsettledModule` note. The refusal counts problems rather than errors, since a note is no error.

=>

export interface TangledFile {
  readonly section: string
  readonly path: Location
  readonly content: string
}

interface Resolved {
  readonly files: ReadonlyArray<TangledFile>
  readonly unwritten: ReadonlyArray<Unwritten>
}

const resolveSinks = (
  modules: Modules,
  entry: Location,
  placeOf: (loom: Location, sink: string) => Effect.Effect<Location>,
  written: WrittenFor,
): Effect.Effect<Resolved> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(entry)),
    Option.match({
      onNone: () => Effect.succeed<Resolved>({ files: [], unwritten: [] }),
      onSome: (module) => {
        const book = bookOf(modules)
        const landing = landingOf(book, modules)
        return Effect.forEach(module.product.files, (file) =>
          placeOf(entry, file.path).pipe(
            Effect.flatMap((path) =>
              written(modules, book, landing, file.code, path).pipe(
                Effect.map((paths) => ({
                  file: {
                    section: file.code.origin.name,
                    path,
                    content: fromCode(modules, file.code, book, specifiersOf(paths)).code,
                  },
                  unwritten: paths.unwritten,
                })),
              ),
            ),
          ),
        ).pipe(
          Effect.map((each) => ({
            files: Array.map(each, (one) => one.file),
            unwritten: Array.flatMap(each, (one) => one.unwritten),
          })),
        )
      },
    }),
  )

const unsettledFailures = (
  modules: Modules,
  entry: Location,
): ReadonlyArray<{ readonly path: Location; readonly diagnostics: ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> }> => {
  const book = bookOf(modules)
  return pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(entry)),
    Option.match({
      onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<Unsettled> => [],
      onSome: (module) =>
        Array.flatMap(module.product.files, (file) => unsettledIn(modules, file.code, book)),
    }),
    Array.dedupeWith(
      (one, other) =>
        one.path === other.path && one.anchor.start.offset === other.anchor.start.offset,
    ),
    Array.groupBy((unsettled) => unsettled.path),
    Record.toEntries,
    Array.map(([path, unsettled]) => ({
      path,
      diagnostics: Array.flatMap(
        unsettled,
        (one) =>
          faulty(
            one.reference
              ? UnsettledModule({ name: one.name, declared: one.declared })
              : UnsettledSection({ name: one.name, declared: one.declared }),
            one.anchor,
          ).diagnostics,
      ),
    })),
  )
}

export class TangleError extends Data.TaggedError('TangleError')<{
  readonly entry: Location
  readonly failures: ReadonlyArray<{
    readonly path: Location
    readonly diagnostics: ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic>
  }>
}> {
  get message(): string {
    const count = this.failures.reduce((n, f) => n + f.diagnostics.length, 0)
    const lines = this.failures.flatMap((f) =>
      f.diagnostics.map((d) => `  ${f.path}:${d.position.start.line}: ${d.message}`),
    )
    return `loom: refusing to tangle ${this.entry} — ${count} problem(s) across the corpus:\n${lines.join('\n')}`
  }
}

## Writing a reference's path

A file that holds a reference needs the reference's path before it is written, and only the language package can write it. The compiler finds the file the reference names with the product pass's `referredFileOf`, places that file and the file being written through `placeOf`, and asks the package for the specifier between the two. `writtenFor` does this for every reference one root's composition holds, which `referencesIn` gathers, and answers with what it `Written`.

A `Written` holds the specifiers it wrote, keyed by the loom and the offset of each reference's anchor, and the references it could not write as `Unwritten`. A package that fails, or no package at all, leaves a reference unwritten, with a `SpecifierUnknown` fault that gives the reason. `specifiersOf` reads a `Written` as the `Specifiers` the projection takes. The tangle refuses a file with an unwritten path, since the file would import nothing, and `byLoom` groups the faults by the loom that holds each reference. The editor writes what it can and leaves the rest, so the language reports the import that has no path. A reference to a module nobody has written yet, or one that a fault already stops, needs no specifier and gets none.

A specifier depends on the file being written, so `writtenFor` takes that file as `from`. The tangle and the composition hand it the path the root tangles to. A root that tangles nowhere is checked beside its loom under a name of its own, so `fileOfRoot` hands in a path in the loom's own folder, the folder the TypeScript service checks such a root in. A corpus that holds no reference writes no specifier, so `rootSpecifiers` asks nothing for it.

=>

interface Unwritten {
  readonly path: Location
  readonly diagnostic: Diagnostic
}

interface Written {
  readonly specifiers: ReadonlyMap<string, string>
  readonly unwritten: ReadonlyArray<Unwritten>
}

type WrittenFor = (
  modules: Modules,
  book: Book,
  landing: Landing,
  root: Code,
  from: Location,
) => Effect.Effect<Written>

const nothingWritten: Written = { specifiers: new Map(), unwritten: [] }

const specifierKey = (owner: Location, at: Position): string => `${owner}#${at.start.offset}`

const writtenOf = (reference: Reference, text: string): Written => ({
  specifiers: new Map([[specifierKey(reference.owner, reference.ref.anchor), text]]),
  unwritten: [],
})

const unwrittenOf = (reference: Reference, reason: string): Written => ({
  specifiers: new Map(),
  unwritten: [
    {
      path: reference.owner,
      diagnostic: faultAt(
        SpecifierUnknown({ name: reference.ref.name, language: reference.host.languageId, reason }),
        reference.ref.anchor,
      ),
    },
  ],
})

const mergeWritten = (one: Written, other: Written): Written => ({
  specifiers: new Map([...one.specifiers, ...other.specifiers]),
  unwritten: [...one.unwritten, ...other.unwritten],
})

const specifiersOf =
  (written: Written): Specifiers =>
  (owner, ref) =>
    Option.fromNullishOr(written.specifiers.get(specifierKey(owner, ref.anchor)))

const byLoom = (
  unwritten: ReadonlyArray<Unwritten>,
): ReadonlyArray<{ readonly path: Location; readonly diagnostics: ReadonlyArray<Diagnostic> }> =>
  pipe(
    unwritten,
    Array.groupBy((one) => one.path),
    Record.toEntries,
    Array.map(([path, each]) => ({ path, diagnostics: Array.map(each, (one) => one.diagnostic) })),
  )

## Locating a source span

The navigation verbs answer in `FrameLocation`s — a path and a line-and-character range — so the editor places a result without re-reading the file. `definitionAt` and `referencesAt` give a `CorpusLocation` in the source's own offsets, since the corpus walks in offsets. `frameLocationOf` converts one to the other, reading the target module's text to turn each offset into a line and a character. `lineCharAt` counts the newlines before an offset for the line, and the distance from the last newline for the character — the zero-based pair the editor speaks. `headingLocationOf` finds a root's section by its name and gives the `FrameLocation` of its heading title, so the composition can point a cross-file `import` at the section that writes the file. A `SummaryEdit` is one edit that keeps a box and its chapter in step, placed the way the editor places it. `lastLineOf` finds the last line of a box, the line that closes it, or the last line of its body when nothing closes it, so the editor folds the box through that line.

=>

export type SummaryEdit = {
  readonly path: Location
  readonly range: FrameLocation['range']
  readonly text: string
}

const lastLineOf = (opened: OpenedBox): LoomWeft =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    Option.orElse(opened.closer, () => Array.last(opened.body)),
    () => opened.opener,
  )

const lineCharAt = (
  text: string,
  offset: number,
): { readonly line: number; readonly character: number } => {
  const before = text.slice(0, offset)
  return {
    line: before.split('\n').length - 1,
    character: offset - (before.lastIndexOf('\n') + 1),
  }
}

const frameLocationOf = (
  modules: Modules,
  location: CorpusLocation,
): Option.Option<FrameLocation> =>
  Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(location.path)?.text), (text) => ({
    path: location.path,
    range: {
      start: lineCharAt(text, location.position.start.offset),
      end: lineCharAt(text, location.position.end.offset),
    },
  }))

const headingLocationOf = (modules: Modules, module: LoomModule, name: string): FrameLocation =>
  pipe(
    Array.findFirst(
      module.doc.sections,
      (section) => normaliseTitle(section.heading.title?.source ?? '') === name,
    ),
    Option.flatMapNullishOr((section) => section.heading.title),
    Option.flatMap((title) =>
      frameLocationOf(modules, { path: module.path, position: title.position }),
    ),
    Option.getOrElse((): FrameLocation => ({
      path: module.path,
      range: { start: { line: 0, character: 0 }, end: { line: 0, character: 0 } },
    })),
  )

## What other files see of a module

An edit to a file can leave every other file as it was. Most edits to a loom change its prose, and no other file reads a loom's prose. Another file reads a module's code, through an anchor or through the files the module tangles, and it finds the module's sections by their titles. Some of its frontmatter matters too. The part and the chapter, with their titles, are how the book's table of contents names the chapter. The package decides where its files land, and the language decides how its code is checked.

`faceOf` reduces a module to that face. It keeps those six frontmatter values, but not a chapter's status or priority, which no other file reads. It keeps the title of every section, the code of every section, and the file each sink tangles to. In the code, an anchor appears as the section it resolves to, or as the name it writes when the product pass left its target open. A reference appears the same way, marked as a module, with the file it names in parentheses. It leaves out the prose, and every position in the source, since an edit to the prose shifts the positions of the code below it without changing the code. Two builds of a module with the same face look the same to every other file.

`rebuild` uses the face to tell an edit that reaches other files from one that does not. It notes the face of the build the cache holds, builds the file again from the source when its text has changed, and compares the two faces. A file the cache did not hold has no face to compare, so `rebuild` reports it as changed.

=>

export const within = (dir: Location, path: Location): boolean => path.startsWith(join(dir, sep))

const tokenValue = (token: { readonly value: string } | undefined): string =>
  Option.getOrElse(
    Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(token), (present) => present.value),
    () => '',
  )

const partFaceOf = (part: Part): string =>
  Match.value(part).pipe(
    Match.when({ type: 'Fragment' }, (fragment) => fragment.text),
    Match.when({ type: 'NameRef' }, (ref) =>
      Option.match(ref.target, { onNone: () => ref.name, onSome: keyOf }),
    ),
    Match.when(
      { type: 'ModuleRef' },
      (ref) =>
        `module ${Option.match(ref.target, { onNone: () => ref.name, onSome: keyOf })} ${Option.match(
          ref.file,
          { onNone: () => '', onSome: (file) => file.name },
        )}`,
    ),
    Match.exhaustive,
  )

const faceOf = (module: LoomModule): string =>
  JSON.stringify({
    frontmatter: Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(module.doc.frontmatter), {
      onNone: () => [],
      onSome: (frontmatter) =>
        Array.map(
          [
            frontmatter.part,
            frontmatter.partName,
            frontmatter.chapter,
            frontmatter.title,
            frontmatter.package,
            frontmatter.language,
          ],
          tokenValue,
        ),
    }),
    titles: Array.map(module.doc.sections, (section) =>
      Option.getOrElse(
        Option.map(Option.fromNullishOr(section.heading.title), (title) => title.source),
        () => '',
      ),
    ),
    code: Array.map(module.product.code, (code) => [
      code.origin.name,
      code.languageId,
      Array.map(code.fragments, partFaceOf),
    ]),
    files: Array.map(module.product.files, (file) => [file.path, file.code.origin.name]),
  })

## Composing only what changed

The editor asks for the composition on nearly every request. The TypeScript service calls `composition` before each hover and each go-to-definition, to bring its program up to date with the book. Composing every file in a large corpus takes tens of milliseconds, and most requests come while nothing has changed. So the compiler keeps the last composition it gave and recomposes only the files a change can reach.

A `Composition` holds what the last call composed. Its `modules` are the corpus that call built, its `placed` give each module the paths its files land at, in the order of its files, and its `files` hold the files each module composed. The compiler keeps one `Composition` for the whole session, starting from `noComposition`, which holds nothing.

`changedSince` names the modules whose files may differ from last time. The memo hands back the very same module object until the module is rebuilt, so a module whose object differs from the kept one has changed. So has a module that was added or removed, or one whose files land somewhere new. `samePlaces` compares two lists of paths, place by place.

A change also reaches the modules that anchor into the changed one, because their files inline its sections. `staleSince` adds them. It asks `dependentsOf` twice, once over the old corpus and once over the new. The old corpus finds a module that anchored a section the change removed. The new corpus finds a module whose anchor the change has just satisfied.

`recompose` builds the next `Composition`. When nothing is stale, it keeps the last composition whole, over the corpus just built. Otherwise it keeps the files of every module that is not stale and composes the rest with `composedFilesOf`, the same composition each file always had, with each reference's path written from the file it lands in. It builds the corpus's `Book` and `Landing` once for all of them, for the open edges `fromProduct` binds and the references it writes. `composition` reads the last composition, recomposes, and keeps the result for the next call. `composedFiles` flattens the result in the corpus's own order, the order `composition` has always returned.

=>

interface Composition {
  readonly modules: Modules
  readonly placed: ReadonlyMap<Location, ReadonlyArray<Location>>
  readonly files: ReadonlyMap<Location, ReadonlyArray<ComposedFile>>
}

const noComposition: Composition = { modules: new Map(), placed: new Map(), files: new Map() }

const composedFilesOf = (
  modules: Modules,
  book: Book,
  landing: Landing,
  module: LoomModule,
  paths: ReadonlyArray<Location>,
  written: WrittenFor,
): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<ComposedFile>> =>
  Effect.forEach(Array.zip(module.product.files, paths), ([file, path]) =>
    written(modules, book, landing, file.code, path).pipe(
      Effect.map(
        (specifiers): ComposedFile => ({
          path,
          content: fromCode(modules, file.code, book, specifiersOf(specifiers)).code,
          loomPath: module.path,
          rootId: file.code.origin.name.toLowerCase(),
          heading: headingLocationOf(modules, module, file.code.origin.name),
        }),
      ),
    ),
  )

const samePlaces = (
  before: ReadonlyArray<Location> | undefined,
  after: ReadonlyArray<Location> | undefined,
): boolean =>
  Option.match(Option.all([Option.fromNullishOr(before), Option.fromNullishOr(after)]), {
    onNone: () => before === after,
    onSome: ([was, now]) =>
      was.length === now.length && Array.every(was, (path, i) => path === now[i]),
  })

const changedSince = (
  previous: Composition,
  modules: Modules,
  placed: ReadonlyMap<Location, ReadonlyArray<Location>>,
): ReadonlyArray<Location> =>
  Array.filter(
    Array.dedupe([...previous.modules.keys(), ...modules.keys()]),
    (path) =>
      previous.modules.get(path) !== modules.get(path) ||
      !samePlaces(previous.placed.get(path), placed.get(path)),
  )

const staleSince = (
  previous: Composition,
  modules: Modules,
  placed: ReadonlyMap<Location, ReadonlyArray<Location>>,
): ReadonlySet<Location> => {
  const changed = changedSince(previous, modules, placed)
  return changed.length === 0
    ? new Set()
    : new Set([
        ...changed,
        ...dependentsOf(previous.modules, changed),
        ...dependentsOf(modules, changed),
      ])
}

const recompose = (
  previous: Composition,
  modules: Modules,
  placed: ReadonlyMap<Location, ReadonlyArray<Location>>,
  written: WrittenFor,
): Effect.Effect<Composition> => {
  const stale = staleSince(previous, modules, placed)
  if (stale.size === 0) return Effect.succeed({ ...previous, modules, placed })
  const book = bookOf(modules)
  const landing = landingOf(book, modules)
  return Effect.forEach(Array.fromIterable(modules.values()), (module) =>
    pipe(
      Option.fromNullishOr(previous.files.get(module.path)),
      Option.filter(() => !stale.has(module.path)),
      Option.match({
        onSome: (kept) => Effect.succeed([module.path, kept] as const),
        onNone: () =>
          composedFilesOf(
            modules,
            book,
            landing,
            module,
            placed.get(module.path) ?? [],
            written,
          ).pipe(Effect.map((files) => [module.path, files] as const)),
      }),
    ),
  ).pipe(Effect.map((pairs) => ({ modules, placed, files: new Map(pairs) })))
}

const composedFiles = (composition: Composition): ReadonlyArray<ComposedFile> =>
  Array.flatten(Array.fromIterable(composition.files.values()))

## The names a root is promised

An anchor may promise names that a section nobody has written yet will provide, as `::[Greeting function]{provides=greet}` does. The code around the anchor uses those names already, so a language service checking it reports each one as undeclared. `promisedNamesIn` gives the service what it needs to lower that error to a warning: every name that an unsettled reference in one root's composition promises.

The service names the root by the id of its embedded document, the root's name lowercased, so `promisedNamesIn` finds the root among the loom's sections by that id. `unsettledIn` walks the root's composition and reports each reference that stops short of code, with the names its anchor promises. Each name becomes a `PromisedName`, paired with the section its anchor names. A name that two anchors promise of one section counts once.

=>

const promisedNamesIn = (
  modules: Modules,
  path: Location,
  rootId: string,
): ReadonlyArray<PromisedName> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)),
    Option.flatMap((module) =>
      Array.findFirst(module.product.code, (code) => code.origin.name.toLowerCase() === rootId),
    ),
    Option.match({
      onNone: (): ReadonlyArray<PromisedName> => [],
      onSome: (root) =>
        pipe(
          unsettledIn(modules, root),
          Array.flatMap((unsettled) =>
            Array.map(unsettled.provides, (provided): PromisedName => ({
              name: provided.name,
              section: unsettled.name,
            })),
          ),
          Array.dedupeWith(
            (one, other) => one.name === other.name && one.section === other.section,
          ),
        ),
    }),
  )

## Projecting a file's tree

`projectTree` assembles a file's virtual-code tree. It emits the file's prose as a Markdown document with `fromProse`, one product document per composition root with `fromProduct`, and gathers them under a `loom` root with `rootVirtualCode` — the source itself, the surface Loom serves the Loom syntax on. It also hands the root the section symbols `symbolMappings` reads from the parse — each heading and anchor span — so go-to-definition and rename reach them on the file the author opened. A root is a section that no same-file anchor pulls into another, and `rootsAt` keeps those names and drops the members. `fromProduct` inlines the members a root pulls in, so each emitted document is a whole resolved Product — and a member's span maps into the root that carries it, rather than standing as a document of its own. The projection follows cross-file `::[…]` references through the corpus modules it is handed, over one `Book` for all the file's roots, so it treats a lone file and a whole corpus the same way. Each root writes its references' paths from the `Specifiers` that `rootSpecifiers` worked out for it.

=>

const namesAt = (modules: Modules, path: Location): ReadonlyArray<string> =>
  pipe(
    Option.fromNullishOr(modules.get(path)),
    Option.match({
      onNone: () => [],
      onSome: (module) => Array.map(module.product.code, (c) => c.origin.name),
    }),
  )

const rootsAt = (modules: Modules, path: Location): ReadonlyArray<string> => {
  const roots = rootNamesAt(modules, path)
  return Array.filter(namesAt(modules, path), (name) => roots.has(name.toLowerCase()))
}

const projectTree = (
  modules: Modules,
  entry: LoomModule,
  specifiers: ReadonlyMap<string, Specifiers>,
): LoomVirtualCode => {
  const book = bookOf(modules)
  return rootVirtualCode(
    entry.text,
    [
      fromProse(modules, entry.path),
      ...pipe(
        rootsAt(modules, entry.path),
        Array.map((name) =>
          fromProduct(
            modules,
            { path: entry.path, name },
            book,
            Option.getOrElse(Option.fromNullishOr(specifiers.get(name)), () => noSpecifiers),
          ),
        ),
      ),
    ],
    symbolMappings(entry.doc),
  )
}

## Crossing to Volar

Every model up to here is plain data, which keeps the pipeline serialisable and easy to test. Volar is not plain data: it wants a function-based `IScriptSnapshot` for a document's text and its own `CodeMapping` for each source span. `toVolar` is the one adapter that crosses from a `LoomVirtualCode` tree to Volar's `VirtualCode`, and the only place in Loom that touches Volar's runtime types. It recurses down the embedded tree, converting every node.

Three small functions do the work. `stringSnapshot` presents a string as the minimal snapshot Volar reads. `featuresOf` chooses which editor features Volar forwards at a mapped span, and for every author-facing token it defers to `LoomSymbol`: it reads the span's symbol kind through `profileOf` and forwards exactly the features that kind allows — a heading navigates, an anchor navigates and verifies, prose only folds and outlines. The table is the one place those features live, so `featuresOf` repeats none of them. It answers only for the two kinds that carry the projection's structure rather than a token a cursor rests on. A `source` span — the whole-file identity mapping the root carries — takes verification and formatting, so Loom's own diagnostics surface on the file, and the Loom language formats the whole file through it. It takes Volar's semantic features too, but tells Volar to highlight nothing there, because Volar forwards an inlay hint only through a semantic span, and the root's colours come from the symbols' own spans. It takes completion as well, so the Loom language can offer the labels an anchor takes between the anchor's braces. It takes structure too, so the Loom language can fold each box under its caption, since Volar forwards a folding range only through a span that carries structure. No other feature fires there. A `product` span carries the full set, verification and completion and semantics among them, so the author edits the resolved composition as if it were the source. `toCodeMapping` rewrites one of our `Mapping`s — a `.loom` source span paired with the generated span it projects to — as Volar's `CodeMapping`, choosing its features with `featuresOf`.

=>

export const stringSnapshot = (text: string): ts.IScriptSnapshot => ({
  getText: (start, end) => text.slice(start, end),
  getLength: () => text.length,
  getChangeRange: () => undefined,
})

const productFeatures: CodeMapping['data'] = {
  verification: true,
  completion: true,
  semantic: true,
  navigation: true,
  structure: true,
}

const featuresOf = (kind: Mapping['kind']): CodeMapping['data'] =>
  Match.value(kind).pipe(
    Match.when('source', () => ({
      verification: true,
      format: true,
      semantic: { shouldHighlight: () => false },
      completion: true,
      structure: true,
    })),
    Match.when('product', () => productFeatures),
    Match.when(Match.defined, (symbol) => profileOf(symbol).features),
    Match.orElse(() => productFeatures),
  )

const toCodeMapping = (m: Mapping): CodeMapping => ({
  sourceOffsets: [m.source.start.offset],
  generatedOffsets: [m.genStart],
  lengths: [m.source.end.offset - m.source.start.offset],
  generatedLengths: [m.genLength],
  data: featuresOf(m.kind),
})

export const toVolar = (vc: LoomVirtualCode): VirtualCode => ({
  id: vc.id,
  languageId: vc.languageId,
  snapshot: stringSnapshot(vc.code),
  mappings: Array.map(vc.mappings, toCodeMapping),
  embeddedCodes: Array.map(vc.embeddedCodes, toVolar),
})

## The document source

`DocumentSource` is the compiler's seam to a workspace's text. It stands in front of the `WorkspaceSource` service, which reaches the storage the looms are kept in, as the chapter "Where a workspace is kept" describes. Its `read` method returns a loom's text by path. When it holds that loom's text from an opened workspace, it answers from memory. Otherwise it asks the `WorkspaceSource` service to read the loom, and fails with `ReadError` when the loom cannot be read. On the file system that read never waits, because the editor projects inside Volar's synchronous hook, which cannot await. The tangler injects this source; the editor injects one of its own.

Its `hold` method keeps the text of each loom it is given, keyed by path, and replaces any text it held for those looms. The language server hands it every loom of the workspace that the `open` method of the `WorkspaceSource` service fetches, as the chapter "Where a workspace is kept" describes. Later the server hands it each loom that storage reports as changed. Its `forget` method drops a loom that storage no longer holds. A read of that loom then goes to the `WorkspaceSource` service and fails, as a read of a deleted file does. In short, the `WorkspaceSource` service fetches, and `DocumentSource` remembers.

It also lists a loom's corpus. `list` asks the `WorkspaceSource` service's `corpusOf` method for the looms that share the loom's corpus, the siblings the compiler builds into one corpus. On the file system the method walks through `loomsIn`, as the tangler does, so every listing of a corpus follows the same rule.

=>

export class DocumentSource extends Context.Service<DocumentSource>()('DocumentSource', {
  make: Effect.gen(function* () {
    const source = yield* WorkspaceSource
    const held = yield* Ref.make<ReadonlyMap<Location, string>>(new Map())
    return {
      read: (path: Location): Effect.Effect<string, ReadError> =>
        Ref.get(held).pipe(
          Effect.flatMap((texts) =>
            Option.match(Option.fromNullishOr(texts.get(path)), {
              onNone: () => source.read(path),
              onSome: (text) => Effect.succeed(text),
            }),
          ),
        ),
      hold: (looms: ReadonlyMap<Location, string>): Effect.Effect<void> =>
        Ref.update(held, (texts) => new Map([...texts, ...looms])),
      forget: (loom: Location): Effect.Effect<void> =>
        Ref.update(held, (texts) => new Map(Array.filter([...texts], ([path]) => path !== loom))),
      list: Option.some((loom: Location): Effect.Effect<ReadonlyArray<Location>> => source.corpusOf(loom)),
    }
  }),
}) {
  static readonly layer = Layer.effect(this, this.make)
}

## What the compiler draws on

From Volar come the `VirtualCode` and `CodeMapping` types the adapter targets. From the pipeline come its collaborators: `LoomCorpusAstBuilder` with its `Source` seam and `ReadError`; the projection functions `fromCode`, `fromProduct`, `fromProse`, `unsettledIn` with the `Unsettled` reference it finds, `contractsIn`, which reads what each anchor says its section provides, `rootNamesAt`, `rootVirtualCode`, and `symbolMappings`; the corpus model — `LoomModule`, the `Book` that a judgement and a projection resolve names through, with `bookOf`, which builds it, the `corpusErrors` and `moduleDiagnostics` readers, `namesakesOf`, which finds the chapters of a part that share a title, `boxNoticesIn` with the `BoxNotice` it gives, which says what each box of a loom that points to a loom needs, `openedBoxesOf` with the `OpenedBox` it gives and `captionOf`, which give each box of a loom and its caption, with the `LoomWeft` a box's lines are, `refreshedSummaries`, which brings every box back in step, and `syncedSummaries`, which carries an edit to a box or its loom across to the other, the `reachable`, `transitiveDependents` and `dependentsOf` reaches over the anchor graph, and the `definitionAt`, `referencesAt`, `renameAt`, `renameRangeAt`, and `navigationRangeAt` navigation walks with the `CorpusLocation` they yield, beside `hintsAt`, which finds the titles numbered references stand for, and `labelSlotAt`, which finds the braces after an anchor that the cursor stands in; the product pass's `crossFileFaults`, which judges an anchor that reaches beyond its loom against the corpus, `compositionCycles`, which faults a composing anchor that loops back to its own section, and `usedSections` and `unusedSections`, which find the code no file includes; the `anchorKinds` from `LoomTokens`, the kinds an anchor takes, which the editor offers as labels; the fault catalog's `faulty`, with the `DuplicateName`, `UnresolvedTocEntry`, `UnsettledSection`, `UnwrittenLoom`, `UnsettledTrope`, and `StaleSummary` notes and faults the compiler raises itself, and `faultAt`, which gives the `MissingSymbol` diagnostic a contract hands the editor; the product model's `Part` and its `keyOf`, which names the section an anchor resolves to; the virtual-code model, `LoomVirtualCode` and its `Mapping`; and the `LoomMemo` cache with the `LoomDiskCache` behind it. The `WeaveBuilder` pass projects the whole corpus into the `WovenCorpus` the `weave` verb returns. The `TableOfContentsBuilder` pass reads the corpus's frontmatter into a table of contents, and `rewriteBook` splices that table back into the book — together what the `contents` verb returns. `declaredKindsOf` and `kindFaults` from the registry gather what tropes declare, each a `DeclaredKind`, and judge each module's boxes against it, `TropePackages` reads the kinds of the tropes a workspace installs, and `boundariesOf` and `boundaryFaults` derive each trope's boundary and judge each module's reaches against them. `profileOf` and `symbolsOf` from `LoomSymbol` give each mapped span its editor features and each of a file's tokens its colour, and `normaliseTitle` folds a heading title to the name an anchor resolves. From the language-service contract come `FrameLocation`, the editor-shaped range the navigation verbs return, `FrameToken`, a range paired with the colour `semanticTokens` returns, `FrameHint`, a position paired with the label `hints` returns, `FrameBox`, a box's lines paired with its caption, which `boxes` returns, `FrameCompletion`, a label paired with the range it replaces and the text it writes there, which `completions` returns, `FrameContract`, which `contracts` returns, and `PromisedName`, which `promised` returns. `Diagnostic` carries an error's message and place, and `Health` the diagnostics a judgement gives. From the `WorkspaceSource` module comes the `WorkspaceSource` service, which `DocumentSource` stands in front of and whose `placeOf` method places a sink. Effect supplies the composition primitives, `Data` among them for the tagged `TangleError`, `Record` for grouping the unsettled references of a tangle, and `Ref` for the composition the compiler keeps, and Bun's `path` tells what lies under a folder.

References add a few more. From the product pass comes what fits a reference to its file: `referredFileOf`, the `Referred` it answers, the `Landing` it reads, `landingOf`, which builds the landing, and `holdsReferences`, which tells whether a corpus refers at all. The projection gives `referencesIn`, which gathers a root's references as `Reference`s, and the `Specifiers` the projection writes them with, `noSpecifiers` among them. The product model gives the `Code` of a root, and `Position` keys a specifier by its anchor's place. The fault catalog gives `UnsettledModule` and `SpecifierUnknown`, and the language-service contract gives the `SpecifierRequest` a package answers and the `ServiceError` it fails with. `LanguagePackages` is the service that asks the packages, which the environment may provide.

=>

import type { CodeMapping, VirtualCode } from '@volar/language-core'
import { Array, Context, Data, Effect, Layer, Match, Option, pipe, Record, Ref } from 'effect'
import { join, sep } from 'node:path'
import type * as ts from 'typescript'
import { type Diagnostic, type Health, type Position } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomNode'
import { type LoomWeft } from '@athrio/loom-ast/Weft'
import { LoomCorpusAstBuilder, type ReadError, type Source } from '#ast/LoomCorpusAstBuilder'
import {
  bookOf,
  type Book,
  BoxNotice,
  boxNoticesIn,
  captionOf,
  openedBoxesOf,
  type OpenedBox,
  corpusErrors,
  definitionAt,
  dependentsOf,
  hintsAt,
  labelSlotAt,
  moduleDiagnostics,
  namesakesOf,
  reachable,
  referencesAt,
  renameAt,
  renameRangeAt,
  navigationRangeAt,
  refreshedSummaries,
  syncedSummaries,
  transitiveDependents,
  unresolvedTocEntriesIn,
  type CorpusLocation,
  type LoomModule,
  type Location,
} from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomCorpusAst'
import {
  DuplicateName,
  faultAt,
  faulty,
  MissingSymbol,
  SpecifierUnknown,
  StaleSummary,
  UnresolvedTocEntry,
  UnwrittenLoom,
  UnsettledTrope,
  UnsettledModule,
  UnsettledSection,
} from '#ast/LoomFault'
import {
  compositionCycles,
  crossFileFaults,
  holdsReferences,
