=>

::[Testing what an edit evicts]

## Testing the chain over looms held in memory

The chain runs from a book to the files its corpus composes. These cases drive it end to end without a disk. The `LoomCompiler.layer` layer supplies the passes and caches the compiler draws on, such as `LoomCorpusAstBuilder` and `LoomMemo`. It leaves two services to its caller, the `DocumentSource` that reads each loom and the `WorkspaceSettings` that settles each loom's build settings. The cases supply a stand-in for each, so the compiler builds a corpus held in memory.

The cases run on Bun's test runner. The `effectify` helper from the `@athrio/effect-test` package adds an `it.effect` function to Bun's `it`, so a case can be written as an Effect, which the runner runs to its end. Each case provides the compiler's layer to its own Effect. Providing a layer builds it afresh, so no case sees a module that another case built.

=>

import { describe, expect, it as it_ } from 'bun:test'
import { effectify } from '@athrio/effect-test'
import { Effect, Layer, Option } from 'effect'
import { DocumentSource, LoomCompiler } from '../src/LoomCompiler'
import { LoomMemo } from '../src/LoomMemo'
import { WorkspaceSettings } from '../src/WorkspaceSettings'
import { LoomDiskCache } from '../src/LoomDiskCache'
import { FileSystemSource } from '../src/WorkspaceSource'
import { defaultAnchorDelims } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomTokens'

const it = effectify(it_)

~

The first corpus holds two looms. The chapter, `chapter.loom`, holds the section `The chapter` with one line of code. The book, `book.loom`, places that section with an anchor that names the chapter's file. A projection of the book alone cannot follow that anchor, but the corpus can. Through the anchor the corpus reaches the chapter, and an edit to the chapter reaches the book.

A loom written into this loom's code would be read as part of this loom. A line that starts with `#` and a space would open a section here. A line that starts with `=>`, or a lone `~`, would open or close a block of code. An anchor counts as an anchor wherever it stands. So each fixture is a list of its lines, and the `lines` helper joins them into the text a file holds, ending on a newline. The `anchor` helper spells an anchor in two pieces for the same reason.

=>

const lines = (...rows: ReadonlyArray<string>): string => `${rows.join('\n')}\n`

const anchor = (name: string): string => `::${'['}${name}]`

const files: Record<string, string> = {
  '/chapter.loom': lines(
    '---',
    'Language: TypeScript',
    '---',
    '',
    '# The chapter',
    '',
    '=>',
    '',
    'export const c = 1',
  ),
  '/book.loom': lines(
    '---',
    'Language: TypeScript',
    '---',
    '',
    '# The part',
    '',
    anchor('The chapter'),
  ),
}

~

The stand-in source reads each loom from the `files` record, which maps a path to the loom's text. It reads a path the record lacks as empty text, lists every path in the record as the corpus, and ignores any loom it is asked to hold or forget.

The stand-in configuration gives every loom the same settings. It keeps Loom's default anchor delimiters and a width of a hundred, and names no primary language and no variables. The layer also provides the real `FileSystemSource` layer, which places each file a loom tangles, and `LoomDiskCache.none`, the build cache that keeps nothing. No folder above a path such as `/chapter.loom` holds a configuration, so each file lands beside its loom, and no case writes to the disk.

=>

const TestDocs = Layer.succeed(DocumentSource, {
  read: (path: string) => Effect.succeed(files[path] ?? ''),
  list: Option.some(() => Effect.succeed(Object.keys(files))),
  hold: () => Effect.void,
  forget: () => Effect.void,
})

const TestConfig = Layer.succeed(WorkspaceSettings, {
  manifest: () => Effect.succeed(Option.none()),
  resolve: () =>
    Effect.succeed({
      delims: defaultAnchorDelims,
      primaryLanguage: undefined,
      variables: {},
      width: 100,
    }),
})

~

The `LoomMemo` cache keeps each module the compiler builds and counts its hits and misses. The cases read those counts, so the `layer` they run under merges the memo's layer beside the compiler's. Effect builds a layer once, however many times one graph names it. The memo a case reads is therefore the very memo the compiler fills.

=>

const layer = Layer.provide(
  Layer.merge(LoomCompiler.layer, LoomMemo.layer),
  Layer.mergeAll(TestDocs, TestConfig, FileSystemSource, LoomDiskCache.none),
)

## What a book reaches, and what the memo keeps

The `reach` verb names the looms an entry draws in. From the book it names the chapter alone, since the book places one section and that section lives in `chapter.loom`.

=>

describe('LoomCompiler — the chain projected for each consumer', () => {
  it.effect('reach reports the chapters a book places', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const c = yield* LoomCompiler
      expect([...(yield* c.reach('/book.loom'))]).toEqual(['/chapter.loom'])
    }).pipe(Effect.provide(layer)),
  )

~

The `invalidate` verb evicts a loom from the cache and names what an edit to that loom must reproject. The case first builds the corpus through `reach`, which loads the book and the chapter into the cache. The case then invalidates the chapter, and `invalidate` names the chapter itself and the book that places it.

=>

  it.effect('invalidate names the file and the books that place it', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const c = yield* LoomCompiler
      yield* c.reach('/book.loom')
      const dirty = yield* c.invalidate('/chapter.loom')
      expect([...dirty].sort()).toEqual(['/book.loom', '/chapter.loom'])
    }).pipe(Effect.provide(layer)),
  )

~

The memo keeps every module the compiler builds, so a second request rebuilds nothing. The first `reach` builds the book and the chapter, two misses that leave two modules in the memo. The second `reach` finds both modules there. The misses stay at two, and the hits grow.

=>

  it.effect('reuses built modules across requests (the memo)', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const c = yield* LoomCompiler
      const memo = yield* LoomMemo
      yield* c.reach('/book.loom')
      const first = yield* memo.stats
      expect(first).toMatchObject({ misses: 2, size: 2 })

      yield* c.reach('/book.loom')
      const second = yield* memo.stats
      expect(second.misses).toBe(2)
      expect(second.hits).toBeGreaterThan(first.hits)
    }).pipe(Effect.provide(layer)),
  )
})

## The composition as a filesystem

The `composition` verb gives the editor each file the corpus composes, with the code a root tangles and the path where the file lands. A language service for the product builds its program over those files. An import from another loom's output then resolves against that loom's live composition, not against the file on disk.

Two looms make this corpus. Each tangles a file of its own, named through the `Package` field of its frontmatter and a bare `{Tangle}` on its heading. The loom `a.loom` tangles `a.ts`, which imports `b` from `./b.js`. The loom `b.loom` tangles `b.ts`, which exports `b`. The `crossLayer` layer serves this corpus through a stand-in source of its own, under the same configuration.

=>

const crossImport: Record<string, string> = {
  '/a.loom': lines(
    '---',
    'Language: TypeScript',
    'Package: a.ts',
    '---',
    '',
    '# A {Tangle}',
    '',
    '=>',
    '',
    "import { b } from './b.js'",
    'export const a = b + 1',
  ),
  '/b.loom': lines(
    '---',
    'Language: TypeScript',
    'Package: b.ts',
    '---',
    '',
    '# B {Tangle}',
    '',
    '=>',
    '',
    'export const b = 2',
  ),
}

const crossLayer = Layer.provide(
  Layer.merge(LoomCompiler.layer, LoomMemo.layer),
  Layer.mergeAll(
    FileSystemSource,
    LoomDiskCache.none,
    Layer.succeed(DocumentSource, {
      read: (path: string) => Effect.succeed(crossImport[path] ?? ''),
      list: Option.some(() => Effect.succeed(Object.keys(crossImport))),
      hold: () => Effect.void,
      forget: () => Effect.void,
    }),
    TestConfig,
  ),
)

~

The case composes from `a.loom` and finds each file by the id of its root. Each file lands at its output path and holds its content. The file of `a` names the loom it came from and the place of its root's heading title. That title, `A`, starts on line 5 at character 2, both counted from zero. A go-to-definition on a cross-file import lands there, on the section that tangles the file.

=>

describe('LoomCompiler — composition projects the de re as a filesystem', () => {
  it.effect('returns every root at its output path, with content and origin', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const c = yield* LoomCompiler
      const files = yield* c.composition('/a.loom')
      const byId = new Map(files.map((file) => [file.rootId, file]))
      const a = byId.get('a')
      const b = byId.get('b')
      expect(a?.path).toBe('/a.ts')
      expect(a?.loomPath).toBe('/a.loom')
      expect(a?.content).toContain("import { b } from './b.js'")
      expect(b?.path).toBe('/b.ts')
      expect(b?.content).toContain('export const b = 2')
      expect(a?.heading.path).toBe('/a.loom')
      expect(a?.heading.range.start).toEqual({ line: 5, character: 2 })
    }).pipe(Effect.provide(crossLayer)),
  )
})

## Recomposing only what an edit reaches

The compiler keeps the last composition it gave and recomposes only the files a change can reach. These cases watch it do so over three looms. The book tangles `book.ts`. Its code inlines the section `The chapter` from `chapter.loom` through a cross-file anchor. The aside tangles `aside.ts` and anchors nothing, so no edit to the chapter reaches it.

The `editable` record holds the three looms where a case can rewrite them. A case edits the chapter there, invalidates the chapter as the editor does, and composes again. The `byRoot` helper keys the composed files by the id of their root.

=>

const editable: Record<string, string> = {
  '/book.loom': lines(
    '---',
    'Language: TypeScript',
    'Package: book.ts',
    '---',
    '',
    '# The book {Tangle}',
    '',
    '=>',
    '',
    anchor('The chapter'),
  ),
  '/chapter.loom': lines(
    '---',
    'Language: TypeScript',
    '---',
    '',
    '# The chapter',
    '',
    '=>',
    '',
    'export const c = 1',
  ),
  '/aside.loom': lines(
    '---',
    'Language: TypeScript',
    'Package: aside.ts',
    '---',
    '',
    '# The aside {Tangle}',
    '',
    '=>',
    '',
    'export const aside = 0',
  ),
}

const editableLayer = Layer.provide(
  Layer.merge(LoomCompiler.layer, LoomMemo.layer),
  Layer.mergeAll(
    FileSystemSource,
    LoomDiskCache.none,
    Layer.succeed(DocumentSource, {
      read: (path: string) => Effect.succeed(editable[path] ?? ''),
      list: Option.some(() => Effect.succeed(Object.keys(editable))),
      hold: () => Effect.void,
      forget: () => Effect.void,
    }),
    TestConfig,
  ),
)

const byRoot = <F extends { readonly rootId: string }>(files: ReadonlyArray<F>) =>
  new Map(files.map((file) => [file.rootId, file]))

~

With nothing changed, a second composition gives back the very objects the first gave, both the book's file and the aside's.

=>

describe('LoomCompiler — composition recomposes only what changed', () => {
  it.effect('gives back the same files when nothing changed', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const c = yield* LoomCompiler
      const first = byRoot(yield* c.composition('/book.loom'))
      const second = byRoot(yield* c.composition('/book.loom'))
      expect(first.get('thebook')).toBeDefined()
      expect(second.get('thebook')).toBe(first.get('thebook'))
      expect(second.get('theaside')).toBe(first.get('theaside'))
    }).pipe(Effect.provide(editableLayer)),
  )

~

The second case changes `c = 1` to `c = 2` in the chapter and invalidates the chapter. The book's file then holds the new value, since the book inlines the chapter. The aside's file stays the same object as before, because nothing it composes has changed.

=>

  it.effect('recomposes a file that inlines an edited section, and keeps the rest', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const c = yield* LoomCompiler
      const before = byRoot(yield* c.composition('/book.loom'))
      expect(before.get('thebook')?.content).toContain('export const c = 1')

      editable['/chapter.loom'] = editable['/chapter.loom']!.replace('c = 1', 'c = 2')
      yield* c.invalidate('/chapter.loom')
      const after = byRoot(yield* c.composition('/book.loom'))

      expect(after.get('thebook')?.content).toContain('export const c = 2')
      expect(before.get('theaside')).toBeDefined()
      expect(after.get('theaside')).toBe(before.get('theaside'))
    }).pipe(Effect.provide(editableLayer)),
  )
})

## The compiler test file [test/LoomCompiler.test.ts]

=>

::[Testing the chain over looms held in memory]

::[What a book reaches, and what the memo keeps]

::[The composition as a filesystem]

::[Recomposing only what an edit reaches]

## Testing an anchor into another loom

An anchor composes a section from another loom by the section's name. The product pass reads one loom at a time and cannot see the other. It records the edge instead, and the compiler judges the edge against the corpus. Outside its own loom, a bare name finds a chapter, and a loom with no frontmatter is a chapter titled by its first heading. The tangle inlines the section when the book holds it, and refuses the build when it does not. The editor shows the inlined block too, and maps none of the block into the loom that anchors it.

Each case builds its corpus in memory from two or three looms. The looms carry no frontmatter and build under TypeScript as the primary language, the shape of Loom's quick start. The cases run on Bun's runner with `effectify`, as the chain's cases do. Effect's `Array` module searches the tree the editor receives.

=>

import { describe, expect, it as it_ } from 'bun:test'
import { effectify } from '@athrio/effect-test'
import { Array, Effect, Layer, Option } from 'effect'
import { DocumentSource, LoomCompiler } from '../src/LoomCompiler'
import { WorkspaceSettings } from '../src/WorkspaceSettings'
import { LoomDiskCache } from '../src/LoomDiskCache'
import { FileSystemSource } from '../src/WorkspaceSource'
import { defaultAnchorDelims } from '@athrio/loom-ast/LoomTokens'

const it = effectify(it_)

~

The `greeting` loom holds the section `Saying hello`, which defines `greet`. Its first heading titles the loom as a chapter, so a bare `::[Saying hello]` reaches it from another loom. The `helloAnchoring` function writes the loom that calls `greet`, a section named `The program` that tangles to `hello.ts`. The program's code opens with the line a case passes in, an anchor to the greeting, and closes by greeting the world. The `program` constant holds what a sound tangle writes, the definition of `greet` inlined above the call. As in the chain's cases, the `lines` and `anchor` helpers keep each fixture from reading as part of this loom.

=>

const lines = (...rows: ReadonlyArray<string>): string => `${rows.join('\n')}\n`

const anchor = (name: string): string => `::${'['}${name}]`

const greeting = lines(
  '# Saying hello',
  '',
  'A greeting takes a name and returns a line of welcome.',
  '',
  '=>',
  'const greet = (name: string): string => `Hello, ${name}!`',
)

const helloAnchoring = (line: string) =>
  lines(
    '# The program [hello.ts]',
    '',
    'The program greets the world.',
    '',
    '=>',
    line,
    "console.log(greet('world'))",
  )

const program = `const greet = (name: string): string => \`Hello, \${name}!\`
console.log(greet('world'))
`

~

The `layerOf` function builds the compiler over a record of looms. Its stand-in source reads the record, and its stand-in configuration names TypeScript as the primary language. The `tangled` function tangles an entry over that layer. The `refusal` function expects the tangle to fail and gives back the message the failure carries.

=>

const layerOf = (files: Record<string, string>) =>
  Layer.provide(
    LoomCompiler.layer,
    Layer.mergeAll(
      FileSystemSource,
      LoomDiskCache.none,
      Layer.succeed(DocumentSource, {
        read: (path: string) => Effect.succeed(files[path] ?? ''),
        list: Option.some(() => Effect.succeed(Object.keys(files))),
        hold: () => Effect.void,
        forget: () => Effect.void,
      }),
      Layer.succeed(WorkspaceSettings, {
        manifest: () => Effect.succeed(Option.none()),
        resolve: () =>
          Effect.succeed({
            delims: defaultAnchorDelims,
            primaryLanguage: 'typescript',
            variables: {},
            width: 100,
          }),
      }),
    ),
  )

const tangled = (files: Record<string, string>, entry: string) =>
  LoomCompiler.pipe(
    Effect.flatMap((c) => c.tangle(entry)),
    Effect.provide(layerOf(files)),
  )

const refusal = (files: Record<string, string>, entry: string) =>
  tangled(files, entry).pipe(
    Effect.flip,
    Effect.map((error) => error.message),
  )

## A section from another loom, inlined or refused

The first case anchors `Saying hello`, the chapter that `greeting.loom` holds beside the program. The tangle writes one file, `hello.ts`, beside the program. The file holds `program` exactly.

=>

describe('a code anchor composes a section from another loom', () => {
  it.effect('tangles the section its name finds in another loom', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const files = yield* tangled(
        {
          '/corpus/greeting.loom': greeting,
          '/corpus/hello.loom': helloAnchoring(anchor('Saying hello')),
        },
        '/corpus/hello.loom',
      )
      expect(files.map((f) => f.path)).toEqual(['/corpus/hello.ts'])
      expect(files[0]!.content).toBe(program)
    }),
  )

~

An anchor that composes, yet writes a loom's path after its name, composes nothing. Only a reference names a file in parentheses, so the tangle refuses the anchor with the `StrayTarget` fault, whose span covers the target, and the fault's one fix removes the target.

=>

  it.effect('refuses a composing anchor that writes a loom’s path', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const files = {
        '/corpus/greeting.loom': greeting,
        '/corpus/hello.loom': helloAnchoring(`${anchor('Saying hello')}(greeting.loom)`),
      }
      expect(yield* refusal(files, '/corpus/hello.loom')).toContain(
        '/corpus/hello.loom:6: Stray target: `(greeting.loom)` names a file',
      )
      const [diagnostic] = yield* LoomCompiler.pipe(
        Effect.flatMap((c) => c.diagnose('/corpus/hello.loom')),
        Effect.provide(layerOf(files)),
      )
      const at = diagnostic!.position
      expect(files['/corpus/hello.loom'].slice(at.start.offset, at.end.offset)).toBe(
        '(greeting.loom)',
      )
      expect(diagnostic!.fixes).toEqual([{ title: 'Remove `(greeting.loom)`', text: '', at }])
    }),
  )

~

The tangle refuses an anchor it cannot satisfy. Its message names the loom and the line where the anchor stands, here line 6 of `hello.loom`. The rest of the message says what is wrong, here that no section is named `Saying goodbye`.

=>

  it.effect('refuses a name the book does not hold', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const message = yield* refusal(
        {
          '/corpus/greeting.loom': greeting,
          '/corpus/hello.loom': helloAnchoring(anchor('Saying goodbye')),
        },
        '/corpus/hello.loom',
      )
      expect(message).toContain(
        '/corpus/hello.loom:6: Unresolved anchor: no section named `Saying goodbye`.',
      )
    }),
  )

~

The section may be written in another language, here JSON, and TypeScript code cannot inline it.

=>

  it.effect('refuses a section in another language', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const message = yield* refusal(
        {
          '/corpus/settings.loom': `# Saying hello {json}\n\n=>\n{ "greeting": "Hello" }\n`,
          '/corpus/hello.loom': helloAnchoring(anchor('Saying hello')),
        },
        '/corpus/hello.loom',
      )
      expect(message).toContain('Cross-language transclusion: `Saying hello` is json')
    }),
  )


~

The `diagnose` verb marks a broken anchor for the editor. For the anchor to `Saying goodbye` it gives one diagnostic, an error whose span covers exactly the name the anchor gives.

=>

  it.effect('diagnose marks the broken anchor in the editor', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const files = {
        '/corpus/greeting.loom': greeting,
        '/corpus/hello.loom': helloAnchoring(anchor('Saying goodbye')),
      }
      const diagnostics = yield* LoomCompiler.pipe(
        Effect.flatMap((c) => c.diagnose('/corpus/hello.loom')),
        Effect.provide(layerOf(files)),
      )
      const hello = files['/corpus/hello.loom']
      expect(
        diagnostics.map((d) => [
          d.severity,
          hello.slice(d.position.start.offset, d.position.end.offset),
        ]),
      ).toEqual([['error', 'Saying goodbye']])
    }),
  )

~

The `compile` verb inlines the greeting into the product the editor sees, so the root `theprogram` generates `program` exactly. The case hands `compile` a source of its own, since the editor hands `compile` the buffer it shows.

Every mapping into the product reads the same text in `hello.loom` as in the product. None starts before the end of the product's first line, the line that holds the inlined greeting. So no span of the greeting's block claims an offset in the loom that anchors it.

=>

  it.effect('the editor inlines the section but maps only the anchoring loom', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const files = {
        '/corpus/greeting.loom': greeting,
        '/corpus/hello.loom': helloAnchoring(anchor('Saying hello')),
      }
      const hello = files['/corpus/hello.loom']
      const root = yield* LoomCompiler.pipe(
        Effect.flatMap((c) =>
          c.compile(
            {
              read: (path) => Effect.succeed(files[path as keyof typeof files] ?? ''),
              list: Option.some(() => Effect.succeed(Object.keys(files))),
            },
            '/corpus/hello.loom',
          ),
        ),
        Effect.provide(layerOf(files)),
      )
      const product = Option.getOrThrow(
        Array.findFirst(root.embeddedCodes ?? [], (code) => code.id === 'theprogram'),
      )
      const generated = product.snapshot.getText(0, product.snapshot.getLength())
      expect(generated).toBe(program)
      expect(product.mappings.length).toBeGreaterThan(0)
      product.mappings.forEach((m) => {
        const source = m.sourceOffsets[0]!
        const gen = m.generatedOffsets[0]!
        expect(hello.slice(source, source + m.lengths[0]!)).toBe(
          generated.slice(gen, gen + (m.generatedLengths?.[0] ?? m.lengths[0]!)),
        )
        expect(gen).toBeGreaterThanOrEqual(generated.indexOf('\n'))
      })
    }),
  )
})

## A name found through the book

A bare name that the anchor's own loom lacks resolves through the book. These cases give the program a small book of chapters, each with the frontmatter that names its part and its title. The `chapter` function writes such a loom from its identity lines and its body.

`Greetings` opens Part I, `Saying things`, and its opening section defines `greet`. `Words` opens with prose alone and keeps `greet` in a later section, `Saying hello`. `Silence` declares its title but holds no section at all. Two chapters called `Setup` stand in two parts, and a chapter called `Hello, world` holds a comma in its title. The `book` function gathers them beside the program, whose code opens with the line a case passes in.

=>

const chapter = (identity: ReadonlyArray<string>, ...body: ReadonlyArray<string>): string =>
  lines('---', ...identity, '---', '', ...body)

const greetCode = 'const greet = (name: string): string => `Hello, ${name}!`'

const book = (line: string): Record<string, string> => ({
  '/corpus/greetings.loom': chapter(
    ['Part I: Saying things', 'Chapter 1: Greetings'],
    '# Greetings',
    '',
    'A greeting takes a name and returns a line of welcome.',
    '',
    '=>',
    greetCode,
  ),
  '/corpus/words.loom': chapter(
    ['Part I, Chapter 2: Words'],
    '# Words',
    '',
    'This chapter gathers what the program says.',
    '',
    '## Saying hello',
    '',
    'A greeting takes a name and returns a line of welcome.',
    '',
    '=>',
    greetCode,
  ),
  '/corpus/silence.loom': chapter(['Part I, Chapter 3: Silence'], 'Nothing is said here.'),
  '/corpus/setup-one.loom': chapter(['Part I, Chapter 4: Setup'], '# Setup', '', '=>', greetCode),
  '/corpus/setup-two.loom': chapter(
    ['Part II: Doing things', 'Chapter 1: Setup'],
    '# Setup',
    '',
    '=>',
    greetCode,
  ),
  '/corpus/hello-world.loom': chapter(
    ['Part I, Chapter 5: Hello, world'],
    '# Hello, world',
    '',
    '=>',
    greetCode,
  ),
  '/corpus/hello.loom': helloAnchoring(line),
})

~

A bare name finds a chapter when the program's own loom holds no section of that name. The anchor composes the chapter's opening section, so `::[Greetings]` inlines `greet` above the call.

=>

describe('a name resolves through the book', () => {
  it.effect('composes the opening section of the chapter a bare name finds', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const files = yield* tangled(book(anchor('Greetings')), '/corpus/hello.loom')
      expect(files[0]!.content).toBe(program)
    }),
  )

~

A path reaches a section of another chapter, from the chapter's title or from its part's name. A bare name does not, because outside its own loom a bare name finds only chapters.

=>

  it.effect('a path names a section of another chapter, directly or through its part', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const direct = yield* tangled(book(anchor('Words, Saying hello')), '/corpus/hello.loom')
      expect(direct[0]!.content).toBe(program)
      const throughPart = yield* tangled(
        book(anchor('Saying things, Words, Saying hello')),
        '/corpus/hello.loom',
      )
      expect(throughPart[0]!.content).toBe(program)
    }),
  )

  it.effect('refuses a bare name that only a section of another chapter carries', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const message = yield* refusal(book(anchor('Saying hello')), '/corpus/hello.loom')
      expect(message).toContain(
        '/corpus/hello.loom:6: Unresolved anchor: no section named `Saying hello`.',
      )
    }),
  )

~

A chapter whose opening section holds no code gives the anchor nothing to compose. The `diagnose` verb warns on the anchor's name, and the tangle refuses the file, since the chapter is still to be settled. A chapter with no section at all gives the anchor no section to compose, so the tangle refuses it too.

=>

  it.effect('warns when the chapter opens with a section that holds no code', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const files = book(anchor('Words'))
      const diagnostics = yield* LoomCompiler.pipe(
        Effect.flatMap((c) => c.diagnose('/corpus/hello.loom')),
        Effect.provide(layerOf(files)),
      )
      const hello = files['/corpus/hello.loom']!
      expect(
        diagnostics.map((d) => [
          d.severity,
          hello.slice(d.position.start.offset, d.position.end.offset),
        ]),
      ).toEqual([['warning', 'Words']])
      expect(yield* refusal(files, '/corpus/hello.loom')).toContain(
        '/corpus/hello.loom:6: To be settled: `Words` holds no code yet, so this anchor composes nothing.',
      )
    }),
  )

  it.effect('refuses a chapter that holds no section', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const message = yield* refusal(book(anchor('Silence')), '/corpus/hello.loom')
      expect(message).toContain(
        '/corpus/hello.loom:6: Unresolved anchor: no section named `Silence`.',
      )
    }),
  )

~

Two chapters may share a title when they stand in different parts. A bare name that finds both cannot choose between them. The fault names the path through each chapter's part, and the `diagnose` verb offers each path as a fix that writes it in place of the name. A path through the part then names the one the author means. A title that holds a comma resolves whole, because a name splits into a path only when the whole name matches nothing.

=>

  it.effect('a title two chapters share is ambiguous until a part names one', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const message = yield* refusal(book(anchor('Setup')), '/corpus/hello.loom')
      expect(message).toContain(
        'Ambiguous anchor: 2 sections are named `Setup`. Write the path to the one you mean:',
      )
      expect(message).toContain('`Saying things, Setup`')
      expect(message).toContain('`Doing things, Setup`')
      const files = yield* tangled(book(anchor('Doing things, Setup')), '/corpus/hello.loom')
      expect(files[0]!.content).toBe(program)
    }),
  )

  it.effect('offers the path through each part as a fix', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const diagnostics = yield* LoomCompiler.pipe(
        Effect.flatMap((c) => c.diagnose('/corpus/hello.loom')),
        Effect.provide(layerOf(book(anchor('Setup')))),
      )
      expect(diagnostics.flatMap((d) => (d.fixes ?? []).map((fix) => fix.text)).sort()).toEqual([
        'Doing things, Setup',
        'Saying things, Setup',
      ])
    }),
  )

  it.effect('a title that holds a comma resolves whole', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const files = yield* tangled(book(anchor('Hello, world')), '/corpus/hello.loom')
      expect(files[0]!.content).toBe(program)
    }),
  )
})

## A name repeated among siblings

A section's name is its identity within its loom, so two sections of one loom may not share it. The `samePage` loom is a chapter headed `# The page` whose one section, `## The page`, tangles `same.html`. Its heading stands on line 6 and the section's on line 10. The tangle refuses the loom with the `DuplicateName` fault on line 10, which names line 6, and writes nothing.

=>

const samePage = chapter(
  ['Part I: Repro', 'Chapter 1: The page'],
  '# The page',
  '',
  "A chapter whose one section carries the chapter's own title.",
  '',
  '## The page [same.html]',
  '',
  '=>',
  '<p>hello from the section</p>',
)

describe('a name repeated among siblings', () => {
  it.effect('refuses a section titled like the chapter heading above it', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const message = yield* refusal({ '/corpus/same.loom': samePage }, '/corpus/same.loom')
      expect(message).toContain(
        '/corpus/same.loom:10: Duplicate name: `The page` is already a section of this loom (line 6). Rename one.',
      )
    }),
  )

~

The `composition` verb is not gated, so it composes the loom even with the fault. It still writes the sink's own code into `same.html`, because it starts from the sink's `Code` rather than finding a section named `The page`.

=>

  it.effect('composes a sink from its own code, never from a namesake', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const files = yield* LoomCompiler.pipe(
        Effect.flatMap((c) => c.composition('/corpus/same.loom')),
        Effect.provide(layerOf({ '/corpus/same.loom': samePage })),
      )
      const page = files.find((file) => file.path.endsWith('same.html'))
      expect(page?.content).toContain('<p>hello from the section</p>')
    }),
  )

~

Two titles collide when they fold to one name, as `Saying hello` and `Saying Hello!` do. The `diagnose` verb marks the later title alone.

=>

  it.effect('faults two titles that fold to one name', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const loom = lines(
        '# Saying hello',
        '',
        '=>',
        'const hello = 1',
        '',
        '# Saying Hello!',
        '',
        '=>',
        'const again = 2',
      )
      const diagnostics = yield* LoomCompiler.pipe(
        Effect.flatMap((c) => c.diagnose('/corpus/twice.loom')),
        Effect.provide(layerOf({ '/corpus/twice.loom': loom })),
      )
      expect(
        diagnostics.map((d) => [
          d.message,
          loom.slice(d.position.start.offset, d.position.end.offset),
        ]),
      ).toEqual([
        [
          'Duplicate name: `Saying Hello!` is already a section of this loom (line 1). Rename one.',
          'Saying Hello!',
        ],
      ])
    }),
  )

~

Chapters are siblings within their part. Two chapters of Part I that share the title `Setup` each carry the fault on their title, naming the other's loom. Two chapters with that title in two different parts carry none, since a path through the part tells them apart, as the cases above show.

=>

  it.effect('faults two chapters of one part that share a title', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const files = {
        '/corpus/setup-one.loom': chapter(
          ['Part I, Chapter 4: Setup'],
          '# Setup',
          '',
          '=>',
          greetCode,
        ),
        '/corpus/setup-three.loom': chapter(
          ['Part I, Chapter 6: Setup'],
          '# Setup',
          '',
          '=>',
          greetCode,
        ),
      }
      const diagnostics = yield* LoomCompiler.pipe(
        Effect.flatMap((c) => c.diagnose('/corpus/setup-one.loom')),
        Effect.provide(layerOf(files)),
      )
      expect(diagnostics.map((d) => d.message)).toEqual([
        'Duplicate name: `Setup` is already a chapter of this part (setup-three.loom). Rename one.',
      ])
    }),
  )
})

## A path by number

A path may name a part or a chapter by its number, the way a loom's identity line does. `Part I, Chapter 2, Saying hello` names the section of `Words` through the numbers alone, and `Saying things, Chapter 1` names the chapter `Greetings` by the part's name and the chapter's number. Each composes the code the titles would.

=>

describe('a path names parts and chapters by number', () => {
  it.effect('composes through a part and a chapter given by number', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const numbered = yield* tangled(
        book(anchor('Part I, Chapter 2, Saying hello')),
        '/corpus/hello.loom',
      )
      expect(numbered[0]!.content).toBe(program)
      const mixed = yield* tangled(book(anchor('Saying things, Chapter 1')), '/corpus/hello.loom')
      expect(mixed[0]!.content).toBe(program)
    }),
  )
})

## A missing section, declared by a fix

An anchor whose name nothing in the book carries offers a fix that declares the section in the anchor's own loom. The heading takes the level of the heading above the anchor, here `#`. With no section after the program, it goes at the end of the loom. With a section after it, `## Later`, it goes just above that section. Either way, the anchor now names a section of its own loom, which holds no code yet, so the loom with the fix applied reports only that the section is to be settled. The second fix labels the anchor `{To be settled}` instead, and the loom then reports that no section has the name yet.

=>

describe('a fix declares a missing section', () => {
  const diagnose = (text: string) =>
    LoomCompiler.pipe(
      Effect.flatMap((c) => c.diagnose('/corpus/hello.loom')),
      Effect.provide(layerOf({ '/corpus/hello.loom': text })),
    )
  const applied = (text: string, fix: { readonly text: string; readonly at?: unknown }) => {
    const at = fix.at as { readonly start: { readonly offset: number } }
    return `${text.slice(0, at.start.offset)}${fix.text}${text.slice(at.start.offset)}`
  }

  it.effect('declares the section below the one that names it', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const alone = helloAnchoring(anchor('Saying goodbye'))
      const followed = `${alone}\n## Later\n\nMore to come.\n`
      const unsettled = (diagnostics: ReadonlyArray<{ readonly message: string }>) =>
        diagnostics.map((d) => d.message)
      const declared =
        'To be settled: `Saying goodbye` holds no code yet, so this anchor composes nothing.'
      const [atEnd, labelled] = (yield* diagnose(alone))[0]!.fixes ?? []
      expect([atEnd!.title, atEnd!.text]).toEqual([
        'Declare the section `Saying goodbye`',
        '\n# Saying goodbye\n',
      ])
      expect(unsettled(yield* diagnose(applied(alone, atEnd!)))).toEqual([declared])
      expect([labelled!.title, labelled!.text]).toEqual([
        'Mark `Saying goodbye` to be settled',
        '{To be settled}',
      ])
      expect(unsettled(yield* diagnose(applied(alone, labelled!)))).toEqual([
        'To be settled: no section is named `Saying goodbye` yet, so this anchor composes nothing.',
      ])
      const [beforeNext] = (yield* diagnose(followed))[0]!.fixes ?? []
      expect(beforeNext!.text).toBe('# Saying goodbye\n\n')
      expect(applied(followed, beforeNext!)).toContain('# Saying goodbye\n\n## Later')
      expect(unsettled(yield* diagnose(applied(followed, beforeNext!)))).toEqual([declared])
    }),
  )
})

## Sections to be settled

A section to be settled holds no code yet. The editor marks the anchor that composes it with a note, since nothing is wrong while the author writes, and the tangle refuses the file the anchor composes into. `settleDiagnose` gives the severity and message of each diagnostic, and `refusal` gives the message a refused tangle carries.

The first case declares `Greeting` below the program, with prose and no code. The anchor on line 6 composes it, so the note says the section holds no code yet, and the tangle refuses the program with that note.

=>

const settleDiagnose = (files: Record<string, string>, entry: string) =>
  LoomCompiler.pipe(
    Effect.flatMap((c) => c.diagnose(entry)),
    Effect.provide(layerOf(files)),
    Effect.map((diagnostics) => diagnostics.map((d) => [d.severity, d.message])),
  )

const declaredLater = `${helloAnchoring(anchor('Greeting'))}\n# Greeting\n\nA greeting, still to be written.\n`

describe('a section to be settled', () => {
  it.effect('marks an anchor that composes a section with no code, and refuses the tangle', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const note = 'To be settled: `Greeting` holds no code yet, so this anchor composes nothing.'
      expect(
        yield* settleDiagnose({ '/corpus/hello.loom': declaredLater }, '/corpus/hello.loom'),
      ).toEqual([['info', note]])
      expect(
        yield* refusal({ '/corpus/hello.loom': declaredLater }, '/corpus/hello.loom'),
      ).toContain(`/corpus/hello.loom:6: ${note}`)
    }),
  )

~

An anchor labelled `{To be settled}` may name a section nobody has declared. The label says the name is meant, so the anchor raises the note, not the `UnresolvedAnchor` fault, and the tangle refuses it all the same.

=>

  it.effect('takes a labelled name nothing carries as meant, and refuses the tangle', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const labelled = {
        '/corpus/hello.loom': helloAnchoring(`${anchor('Greeting')}{To be settled}`),
      }
      const note =
        'To be settled: no section is named `Greeting` yet, so this anchor composes nothing.'
      expect(yield* settleDiagnose(labelled, '/corpus/hello.loom')).toEqual([['info', note]])
      expect(yield* refusal(labelled, '/corpus/hello.loom')).toContain(
        `/corpus/hello.loom:6: ${note}`,
      )
    }),
  )

~

An anchor that states what its section provides promises the section, as the label does. With no section of that name, the anchor raises the same note, not the `UnresolvedAnchor` fault, and the tangle refuses it all the same.

=>

  it.effect('takes the names an anchor says its section provides as a promise', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const promised = {
        '/corpus/hello.loom': helloAnchoring(`${anchor('Greeting')}{provides=greet}`),
      }
      const note =
        'To be settled: no section is named `Greeting` yet, so this anchor composes nothing.'
      expect(yield* settleDiagnose(promised, '/corpus/hello.loom')).toEqual([['info', note]])
      expect(yield* refusal(promised, '/corpus/hello.loom')).toContain(
        `/corpus/hello.loom:6: ${note}`,
      )
    }),
  )

~

Once the section holds code, the label has nothing left to say. The anchor carries the `SettledLabel` note, whose fix removes the label, and the loom with the fix applied reports nothing.

=>

  it.effect('notes a label its section has outgrown, and its fix removes it', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const settled = `${helloAnchoring(`${anchor('Saying hello')}{To be settled}`)}\n${greeting}`
      const [note] = yield* LoomCompiler.pipe(
        Effect.flatMap((c) => c.diagnose('/corpus/hello.loom')),
        Effect.provide(layerOf({ '/corpus/hello.loom': settled })),
      )
      expect([note!.severity, note!.message]).toEqual([
        'info',
        'Settled: `Saying hello` holds code now, so its `{To be settled}` label has nothing left to say.',
      ])
      const [fix] = note!.fixes ?? []
      const at = fix!.at!
      const removed = `${settled.slice(0, at.start.offset)}${fix!.text}${settled.slice(at.end.offset)}`
      expect(removed).toContain(`${anchor('Saying hello')}\n`)
      expect(
        yield* settleDiagnose({ '/corpus/hello.loom': removed }, '/corpus/hello.loom'),
      ).toEqual([])
    }),
  )

~

Beside other parameters, the kind is written in full. The anchor still marks its name as meant, and once the section holds code, the fix removes the `kind` parameter alone, so what the anchor says the section provides stays.

=>

  it.effect('reads the kind in full beside other parameters', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const full = `${anchor('Saying hello')}{kind="To be settled" provides=greet}`
      const labelled = { '/corpus/hello.loom': helloAnchoring(full) }
      expect(yield* settleDiagnose(labelled, '/corpus/hello.loom')).toEqual([
        [
          'info',
          'To be settled: no section is named `Saying hello` yet, so this anchor composes nothing.',
        ],
      ])
      const settled = `${helloAnchoring(full)}\n${greeting}`
      const [note] = yield* LoomCompiler.pipe(
        Effect.flatMap((c) => c.diagnose('/corpus/hello.loom')),
        Effect.provide(layerOf({ '/corpus/hello.loom': settled })),
      )
      const [fix] = note!.fixes ?? []
      const at = fix!.at!
      const removed = `${settled.slice(0, at.start.offset)}${fix!.text}${settled.slice(at.end.offset)}`
      expect(removed).toContain(`${anchor('Saying hello')}{provides=greet}\n`)
    }),
  )
})

## Completing an anchor's label

The `completions` verb offers the labels an anchor takes while the cursor stands between the anchor's braces. The `complete` function asks for them at an offset in a loom that anchors `Greeting` on line 6, and the `at` function writes a range on that line. The anchor, `::[Greeting]`, takes twelve characters, so its braces open at character 12.

The verb offers both kinds an anchor takes, `To be settled` and `Reference`, and leaves it to the editor to narrow them to what the author typed. Between closed braces, each offer replaces the label typed so far, `To b`, from character 13 to character 17. After a brace the author has only opened, the offer writes the label and the closing brace at the cursor. Anywhere else, as on the anchor's name, the verb offers nothing.

=>

const complete = (text: string, offset: number) =>
  LoomCompiler.pipe(
    Effect.flatMap((c) => c.completions('/corpus/hello.loom', offset)),
    Effect.provide(layerOf({ '/corpus/hello.loom': text })),
  )

const at = (from: number, to: number) => ({
  start: { line: 5, character: from },
  end: { line: 5, character: to },
})

describe("completing an anchor's label", () => {
  it.effect('offers the label between the braces, and closes braces left open', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const closed = helloAnchoring(`${anchor('Greeting')}{To b}`)
      expect(yield* complete(closed, closed.indexOf('To b') + 4)).toEqual([
        { label: 'To be settled', range: at(13, 17), text: 'To be settled' },
        { label: 'Reference', range: at(13, 17), text: 'Reference' },
      ])
      const open = helloAnchoring(`${anchor('Greeting')}{`)
      expect(yield* complete(open, open.indexOf('{') + 1)).toEqual([
        { label: 'To be settled', range: at(13, 13), text: 'To be settled}' },
        { label: 'Reference', range: at(13, 13), text: 'Reference}' },
      ])
      expect(yield* complete(closed, closed.indexOf('Greeting') + 2)).toEqual([])
    }),
  )
})

## Brackets after an anchor

An anchor takes no sink, so square brackets right after one belong to the code. The program indexes what it composes, `::[Saying hello][0]`, and the tangle keeps the index after the composed definition. Nothing in the loom is at fault.

=>

describe('brackets after an anchor', () => {
  it.effect('stay code, so an index survives the tangle', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const indexed = {
        '/corpus/hello.loom': `${helloAnchoring(`${anchor('Saying hello')}[0]`)}\n${greeting}`,
      }
      expect(yield* settleDiagnose(indexed, '/corpus/hello.loom')).toEqual([])
      const [file] = yield* tangled(indexed, '/corpus/hello.loom')
      expect(file!.content).toContain('`Hello, ${name}!`[0]\n')
    }),
  )
})

## What an anchor says its section provides

The `contracts` verb gives what an anchor says its section provides, for the editor to compare with what the section's language reports. The program states that `Saying hello` provides `greet` and `farewell`. The contract names the loom that holds the section, where its code begins, on line 13, and its language. Each name carries the diagnostic the editor raises if the section does not declare it. A section to be settled holds no code yet, so an anchor that states names for it holds no contract.

=>

const contractsOf = (text: string) =>
  LoomCompiler.pipe(
    Effect.flatMap((c) => c.contracts('/corpus/hello.loom')),
    Effect.provide(layerOf({ '/corpus/hello.loom': text })),
  )

describe('what an anchor says its section provides', () => {
  it.effect('names what the section must declare, and nothing to be settled', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const stated = `${helloAnchoring(`${anchor('Saying hello')}{provides=[greet, farewell]}`)}\n${greeting}`
      const [contract] = yield* contractsOf(stated)
      expect(contract!.target.path).toBe('/corpus/hello.loom')
      expect(contract!.target.range.start).toEqual({ line: 13, character: 0 })
      expect(contract!.language).toBe('typescript')
      expect(contract!.expected.map((expected) => expected.name)).toEqual(['greet', 'farewell'])
      expect(contract!.expected[1]!.diagnostic.message).toBe(
        'Missing symbol: `Saying hello` declares no `farewell`, which this anchor says it provides.',
      )
      const waiting = helloAnchoring(
        `${anchor('Saying hello')}{kind="To be settled" provides=greet}`,
      )
      expect(yield* contractsOf(waiting)).toEqual([])
    }),
  )
})

## What a program is promised

A language service asks the compiler which names a root is promised, so it can lower its error for each one to a warning. The program promises `greet` of `Greeting`, a section nobody has written, so its root is promised `greet`. Once the program anchors `Saying hello` instead, a section the loom holds with its code written, nothing waits to be settled, and the root is promised nothing.

=>

const promisedOf = (text: string) =>
  LoomCompiler.pipe(
    Effect.flatMap((c) => c.promised('/corpus/hello.loom', 'theprogram')),
    Effect.provide(layerOf({ '/corpus/hello.loom': text })),
  )

describe('what a program is promised', () => {
  it.effect('names what an unsettled section is promised, and nothing once it is written', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      expect(yield* promisedOf(helloAnchoring(`${anchor('Greeting')}{provides=greet}`))).toEqual([
        { name: 'greet', section: 'Greeting' },
      ])
      const settled = `${helloAnchoring(`${anchor('Saying hello')}{provides=greet}`)}\n${greeting}`
      expect(yield* promisedOf(settled)).toEqual([])
    }),
  )
})

## A section composed into itself

A section may not compose itself, however far down, since its code could never be written out whole. The `looping` loom tangles `main.ts` from a section that composes `A`, while `A` composes `B` and `B` composes `A` again. The `diagnose` verb marks both anchors of the loop, on lines 10 and 16, and each fault names the loop from where its anchor stands. The tangle refuses the loom. `cycles` keeps the line and the message of each fault a loop raises.

=>

const cycles = (files: Record<string, string>, entry: string) =>
  LoomCompiler.pipe(
    Effect.flatMap((c) => c.diagnose(entry)),
    Effect.provide(layerOf(files)),
    Effect.map((found) =>
      found
        .filter((d) => d.message.startsWith('Circular composition'))
        .map((d) => ({ line: d.position.start.line, message: d.message })),
    ),
  )

const looping = lines(
  '# Main [main.ts]',
  '',
  '=>',
  anchor('A'),
  '',
  '## A',
  '',
  '=>',
  'const a = 1',
  anchor('B'),
  '',
  '## B',
  '',
  '=>',
  'const b = 2',
  anchor('A'),
)

describe('a section composed into itself', () => {
  it.effect('faults each anchor of two sections that compose each other', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const files = { '/corpus/main.loom': looping }
      expect(yield* cycles(files, '/corpus/main.loom')).toEqual([
        {
          line: 10,
          message:
            'Circular composition: `A` → `B` → `A`. Remove this anchor, or the one in `B`.',
        },
        {
          line: 16,
          message:
            'Circular composition: `B` → `A` → `B`. Remove this anchor, or the one in `A`.',
        },
      ])
      expect(yield* refusal(files, '/corpus/main.loom')).toContain(
        '/corpus/main.loom:16: Circular composition',
      )
    }),
  )

~

A section that composes itself directly is the shortest loop, and its fault says so.

=>

  it.effect('faults a section that composes itself', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const files = {
        '/corpus/main.loom': lines(
          '# Main [main.ts]',
          '',
          '=>',
          anchor('A'),
          '',
          '## A',
          '',
          '=>',
          anchor('A'),
        ),
      }
      expect(yield* cycles(files, '/corpus/main.loom')).toEqual([
        {
          line: 9,
          message: 'Circular composition: `A` composes itself. Remove this anchor.',
        },
      ])
    }),
  )

~

A loop may run through several looms. Each loom here is a chapter titled by its first heading, and each composes the other by that title.

=>

  it.effect('faults a loop that runs through two looms', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const files = {
        '/corpus/ping.loom': lines('# Pinging [ping.ts]', '', '=>', anchor('Ponging')),
        '/corpus/pong.loom': lines('# Ponging', '', '=>', anchor('Pinging')),
      }
      expect((yield* cycles(files, '/corpus/ping.loom')).map((fault) => fault.message)).toEqual([
        'Circular composition: `Pinging` → `Ponging` → `Pinging`. Remove this anchor, or the one in `Ponging`.',
      ])
    }),
  )

~

Composing one section twice is no loop. `A` and `B` both compose `C`, and the tangle writes `C` once in each place. A reference composes nothing, so two sections that refer to each other raise nothing either.

=>

  it.effect('leaves a section composed in two places alone', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const files = {
        '/corpus/main.loom': lines(
          '# Main [main.ts]',
          '',
          '=>',
          anchor('A'),
          anchor('B'),
          '',
          '## A',
          '',
          '=>',
          anchor('C'),
          'const a = 1',
          '',
          '## B',
          '',
          '=>',
          anchor('C'),
          'const b = 2',
          '',
          '## C',
          '',
          '=>',
          'const c = 0',
        ),
      }
      expect(yield* cycles(files, '/corpus/main.loom')).toEqual([])
      const [file] = yield* tangled(files, '/corpus/main.loom')
      expect(file?.content.split('const c = 0').length).toBe(3)
    }),
  )

  it.effect('leaves two sections that refer to each other alone', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const files = {
        '/corpus/main.loom': lines(
          '# Main [main.ts]',
          '',
          '=>',
          `${anchor('Helper')}{Reference}`,
          'const main = 1',
          '',
          '## Helper [helper.ts]',
          '',
          '=>',
          `${anchor('Main')}{Reference}`,
          'const helper = 1',
        ),
      }
      expect(yield* cycles(files, '/corpus/main.loom')).toEqual([])
    }),
  )
})

## Code that no file includes

A section whose code no file includes is never used, and its heading carries a warning. A section an anchor composes is used, and so is one that writes a file. A corpus that writes no file at all is documentation, so its code raises nothing, and neither does the code of an unfinished chapter, one marked `Canvas` or `Planned`, nor a chapter written in prose. `unused` keeps the line and the message of each such warning.

=>

const unused = (files: Record<string, string>, entry: string) =>
  LoomCompiler.pipe(
    Effect.flatMap((c) => c.diagnose(entry)),
    Effect.provide(layerOf(files)),
    Effect.map((found) =>
      found
        .filter((d) => d.message.includes('is never used'))
        .map((d) => ({ line: d.position.start.line, message: d.message })),
    ),
  )

const withHelper = (main: ReadonlyArray<string>) =>
  lines(...main, '', '## Helper', '', '=>', 'const help = 1')

describe('code that no file includes', () => {
  it.effect('warns of a section whose code no file includes', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const files = {
        '/corpus/main.loom': withHelper(['# Main [main.ts]', '', '=>', 'const main = 1']),
      }
      expect(yield* unused(files, '/corpus/main.loom')).toEqual([
        { line: 6, message: '`Helper` is never used across the corpus.' },
      ])
    }),
  )

  it.effect('counts a section an anchor composes as used', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const files = {
        '/corpus/main.loom': withHelper(['# Main [main.ts]', '', '=>', anchor('Helper')]),
      }
      expect(yield* unused(files, '/corpus/main.loom')).toEqual([])
    }),
  )

  it.effect('judges nothing in a corpus that writes no file', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const files = { '/corpus/main.loom': withHelper(['# Main', '', '=>', 'const main = 1']) }
      expect(yield* unused(files, '/corpus/main.loom')).toEqual([])
    }),
  )

  it.effect('passes over an unfinished chapter', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const files = {
        '/corpus/main.loom': lines('# Main [main.ts]', '', '=>', 'const main = 1'),
        '/corpus/sketch.loom': withHelper(['---', 'Status: Canvas', '---', '', '# Sketch']),
      }
      expect(yield* unused(files, '/corpus/sketch.loom')).toEqual([])
    }),
  )

  it.effect('passes over a chapter written in prose', () =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const files = {
        '/corpus/main.loom': lines('# Main [main.ts]', '', '=>', 'const main = 1'),
        '/corpus/genesis.loom': withHelper(['---', 'Language: Prose', '---', '', '# Genesis']),
      }
      expect(yield* unused(files, '/corpus/genesis.loom')).toEqual([])
    }),
  )
})

## The cross-file anchor test file [test/CrossFileAnchor.test.ts]

=>

::[Testing an anchor into another loom]

::[A section from another loom, inlined or refused]

::[A name found through the book]

::[A name repeated among siblings]

::[A path by number]

::[A missing section, declared by a fix]

::[Sections to be settled]

::[Completing an anchor's label]

::[Brackets after an anchor]

::[What an anchor says its section provides]

::[What a program is promised]

